docs/appendix2_crosswalk.md:1:# Multimedia Appendix 2. Year-specific KCHS↔NHANES harmonization crosswalk and the locked predictor-set amendment
docs/appendix2_crosswalk.md:3:Supports Methods §2.5 and §2.5.1. Section A documents how each harmonized variable was resolved in every KCHS survey year and in each NHANES cycle. Section B reproduces, in English, the predictor-set amendment that was written and locked before any model was fitted; the original Korean document (dated 2026-07-15) is included unchanged in the code release.
docs/appendix2_crosswalk.md:82:The original protocol pre-specified 14 harmonized predictors (protocol Table 1). The supervising investigator approved a re-selection of predictors; the investigators elected to keep the external-validation design unchanged (develop in KCHS, apply to NHANES without refitting) and to expand only the set of variables that both surveys share. Sections §2.4 (outcome definition), §2.8 (model development) and §2.10 (external-validation strategy) of the protocol were therefore left unchanged; only the predictor list was amended.
docs/appendix2_crosswalk.md:90:Employment (`soa_01z1` ↔ `OCD150`), self-rated oral health (`ora_01z1` ↔ `OHQ845`), and household food insecurity (`nue_01z1` ↔ `FSDAD`) were the three concepts adopted. Harmonized definitions are given in Table A.2.
docs/manuscript_17var.md:44:Retrospective secondary-data study developing a clinical prediction model with external validation; reported per TRIPOD+AI (2024) [4], risk of bias self-assessed with PROBAST [5]. The study protocol and statistical analysis plan were written and dated before the analysis, and the predictor-set expansion described in §2.5 is a documented amendment that was likewise locked before any model was fitted (§2.5.1). Multimedia Appendix 2 provides the year-specific harmonization crosswalk together with an English rendition of the locked amendment; the unmodified original protocol, statistical analysis plan, and amendment document will be deposited with the analysis code, hyperparameter grids, and model card in a public repository (DOI [to be inserted]; see Data Availability).
docs/manuscript_17var.md:57:Only variables definable identically in both surveys entered the harmonized model. The final harmonized set comprised **17 predictors** (Table 1): the 14 protocol-specified predictors—age, sex, education, household income (within-survey quintiles), self-rated health, partnership, living alone, smoking, alcohol, physical activity, sleep duration, BMI, hypertension, diabetes—plus three added predictors: **employment** (KCHS soa_01z1 ↔ NHANES OCD150), **self-rated oral health** (ora_01z1 ↔ OHQ845), and **household food insecurity** (nue_01z1 ↔ FSDAD). Concept-level harmonization used a label-based per-year crosswalk on the KCHS side and cycle-specific code branching on the NHANES side; self-rated health was harmonized to NHANES HUQ010 (correcting a Table 1 file-code error for HSD010/HSQ).
docs/manuscript_17var.md:59:**2.5.1 Predictor-set amendment and selection provenance.** The three added predictors were identified by crosswalking all 616 KCHS concepts against the NHANES codebook for both cycles (a 175-variable extract of candidate items at the time of the amendment; a later, exhaustive extraction identified 265 variables common to both cycles). The amendment was written and locked before any model was fitted (Multimedia Appendix 2) and required a candidate to be (i) available in both NHANES cycles, (ii) collected in more than one KCHS survey year, and (iii) not circular with the PHQ-9 outcome. Employment, self-rated oral health, and household food insecurity were the three concepts adopted. Candidates rejected at this stage failed on single-year collection (e.g., dyslipidemia, osteoporosis, arthritis), concept mismatch (KCHS records only *awareness of early warning signs* for stroke and myocardial infarction, not physician diagnosis), or absence from the NHANES pre-pandemic cycle (functional limitation). Criteria (i)–(iii) use survey documentation and variable availability only; **no NHANES outcome data entered the amendment**. Each added predictor has an established association with depression in the epidemiological literature—food insecurity (pooled odds ratio ≈1.4) [13], unemployment (≈1.9–2.1) [14], and poor oral health (a bidirectional relationship) [15]—supporting biological and social plausibility rather than data-driven selection. Circular/outcome-contaminated variables (PHQ items, suicidal ideation, EQ-5D anxiety/depression, happiness, perceived stress, mental-health service use including NHANES HUQ090) were pre-specified for exclusion.
docs/manuscript_17var.md:63:**Harmonization cautions.** Alcohol, physical activity, and income use different instruments and were reduced to conservatively comparable definitions (residuals quantified in sensitivity). BMI is self-reported in KCHS, measured in NHANES. Living-alone is unavailable in the NHANES pre-pandemic DEMO file (100% missing) and is retained as missing (not imputed to a constant) to avoid fabricating a constant. Several KCHS items are also structurally missing for whole survey years because the module rotates: food insecurity was not fielded in 2020–2021 (22.1% missing overall), sleep duration was not fielded in 2023 (11.3%), and BMI could be neither read nor derived in 2019 (13.1%). These gaps are year-structural rather than respondent-level, are preserved as missing rather than imputed to a constant, and are addressed by the missing-data strategy (§2.6) and by leave-out and imputation sensitivity analyses (§2.12). Food insecurity additionally uses a single KCHS item versus the 10-item NHANES module. The full year-by-year variable map is provided as Multimedia Appendix 2.
docs/manuscript_17var.md:84:Pre-specified and additional analyses (exact values in Multimedia Appendix 3, Table S7): outcome cut-off (≥5 as well as ≥10, evaluated internally and in both external cohorts), removal of the three added predictors (→14), removal of food insecurity only (→16), weighting approach (weighted vs unweighted training), alternative imputation (missing indicators; multiple imputation by chained equations), and an age lower bound of 30 years applied to the training *and* evaluation samples. The self-report (KCHS) versus measured (NHANES) difference in BMI is inherent to the data sources and is addressed under harmonization cautions (§2.5) and Limitations rather than by swapping measurements. All analyses were performed in Python 3.14 (scikit-learn 1.9.0, lightgbm 4.6.0, shap 0.52.0, numpy 2.4.5, pandas 3.0.3, scipy 1.18.0, pyreadstat 1.3.5, matplotlib 3.10.9); complex-survey weighting, the stratified cluster bootstrap, calibration measures, and decision-curve net benefit were implemented directly rather than through a survey-analysis package, and the implementations are released with the code.
docs/manuscript_17var.md:97:Weighted depression prevalence (PHQ-9 ≥10): KCHS 3.6%, NHANES pre 8.4%, post 12.2% (Figure 1). The surveys differed markedly on measured covariates—e.g., mean BMI 23.7 (KCHS) vs 29.8 (NHANES), and weighted food-insecurity prevalence 2.9% vs ~16%—consistent with genuinely distinct populations and, for food insecurity, with the single-item-vs-module measurement difference. DPQ item non-response was substantial in both NHANES cycles and much larger after the pandemic: among adults ≥20 years, 14.3% of the pre-pandemic and 32.7% of the post-pandemic respondents left three or more PHQ-9 items unanswered, yielding the complete-case analytic samples of 7,888 and 5,210. Non-response was differential—higher among respondents with less than high-school education (20.6% pre, 47.3% post) and among Asian and Black respondents—so the external cohorts, and particularly the post-pandemic fairness estimates, should be read as complete-case results. Reassuringly, adding respondents missing only one or two items using their person-mean changed weighted prevalence by ≤0.1 percentage points in both cycles. Exact values are tabulated in Multimedia Appendix 3, Table S6.
docs/manuscript_17var.md:103:See Table 2 and Figures 2 and 3. Discrimination remained clinically useful but lower: pre **0.772 (0.745–0.797)**, post **0.769 (0.747–0.789)**. Calibration deteriorated in both directions of the calibration line: the slope fell to 0.66 (pre) and 0.68 (post), and CITL rose to +0.49 and +1.11, indicating that the Korea-trained model systematically *under*-predicted absolute risk in the US—as expected from the large prevalence difference (KCHS 3.6% vs NHANES 8.4% and 12.2%). Out-of-sample intercept/slope recalibration restored calibration (slope→0.99, CITL→0.00) with discrimination essentially unchanged — **H2 and H3 supported**.
docs/manuscript_17var.md:105:**Table 2. Discrimination and calibration (primary model, LightGBM, 17 predictors, weighted).**
docs/manuscript_17var.md:128:We localized the ceiling with an **in-domain oracle**: fitting NHANES on its own labels (a deliberate §2.10 violation, for diagnosis only) under cross-validation. Across logistic, penalized-logistic, histogram- and tree-gradient-boosting learners, the honest 5-fold cross-validated oracle reached only ~0.77 (approximately 0.765–0.775 depending on learner; pooled bootstrap 95% CI ≈0.75–0.79), and even the optimistic in-sample (apparent) bound was 0.773. The KCHS-transferred model (pre 0.772, post 0.769) therefore already **sits within the maximum discrimination extractable from these predictors within NHANES itself**—the transported model is at, not below, its achievable ceiling. Matching sample size does not close the gap: reducing the Korean data to the NHANES sample size and cross-validating within it still yielded 0.823 at n=7,888 (the pre-pandemic cohort size) and 0.810 at n=13,098 (the pooled oracle size), rising to 0.830 only at n=200,000 (best of three learners, 5-fold weighted AUROC). At identical n, in other words, the same 17 predictors carry 0.81–0.82 of discrimination in Korea against an NHANES ceiling of ~0.77. Three further checks converged on the same conclusion: (i) covariate-shift correction by density-ratio reweighting of the KCHS training sample toward the NHANES covariate distribution (labels unused) *reduced* external AUROC (to 0.766/0.758), indicating the gap is not covariate shift; (ii) increasing the KCHS training set from 0.5M to 2M examples did not raise—and slightly lowered—external discrimination, consistent with source-specific overfitting rather than an estimation-limited ceiling; and (iii) adding every remaining harmonized-but-unused survey item (public-assistance receipt, alcohol-consumption detail, weekend sleep) left external AUROC unchanged (Δ<0.001). Deep tabular learners (a multilayer perceptron, TabNet, and an FT-Transformer) were additionally trained and transferred; all fell below gradient boosting internally and none exceeded it on both external cohorts (Multimedia Appendix 4), consistent with the model-agnostic ceiling. The residual gap thus reflects **concept shift** (predictor–outcome relationships differ between populations), not sample size, model choice, covariate imbalance, or omitted harmonized information, and is not closable by recalibration (which corrects calibration, not rank ordering).
docs/manuscript_17var.md:131:Predictors were entered in descending order of global SHAP importance and the model refitted at each item count (internal / external-pre / external-post): 3 items 0.782/0.713/0.726; 5 items 0.809/0.741/0.745; 6 items 0.815/0.752/0.754; 7 items 0.819/0.761/0.758; **8 items 0.825/0.770/0.753**; 12 items 0.831/0.771/0.767; 17 items 0.832/0.772/0.769. Eight items was the smallest count whose internal discrimination came within 0.01 AUROC of the full model (0.825 vs 0.832; 7 items fell short at 0.819), and this criterion used internal data only. The resulting 8-item model (self-rated health, age, sex, sleep duration, oral health, partnership, employment, smoking) retained essentially all external discrimination in the pre-pandemic cohort (0.770 vs 0.772) but lost 0.016 in the post-pandemic cohort (0.753 vs 0.769); **H4 is therefore supported for the pre-pandemic cohort and only partially for the post-pandemic cohort**, where a 12-item version recovers the difference (0.767). The 8-item version remains suitable for a low-burden self-administered form, with the post-pandemic shortfall reported as a deployment caveat. The full item-count trajectory is tabulated in Multimedia Appendix 3, Table S8.
docs/manuscript_17var.md:134:Discrimination was stable across specifications that alter modeling choices rather than the target condition (Figure 4): weighted training (0.831/0.773/0.764 internal/pre/post), missing-indicator imputation (0.833/0.770/0.767), multiple imputation by chained equations (0.833/0.770/0.767), and the 16- and 14-predictor variants (§3.4) all lay within 0.01 of the primary model internally. Restricting both training and evaluation to respondents aged ≥30 years *improved* discrimination (0.840/0.784/0.788), consistent with the lower subgroup performance observed at ages 20–39 (§3.8). Changing the outcome definition to PHQ-9 ≥5 lowered discrimination throughout (0.759/0.736/0.739) at a much higher weighted prevalence (16.1%/25.0%/32.1%), as expected when the target shifts from moderate-or-worse symptoms to any symptom burden; notably, the Korea–US gap almost disappears at this cut-off (0.759 vs 0.736–0.739), indicating that the transportability loss is concentrated in the more severe end of the symptom distribution. Data-integrity fixes (§2.13) did not alter substantive conclusions. Exact values are tabulated in Multimedia Appendix 3, Table S7.
docs/manuscript_17var.md:137:Global SHAP importance was dominated by self-rated health (mean |SHAP| 0.58), followed by age (0.38), sex (0.30), sleep duration (0.26), **self-rated oral health (0.24)**, partnership (0.17), and **employment (0.17)**; food insecurity contributed modestly (0.06, ranked 11th). Two of the three added predictors (oral health, employment) thus rank within the top seven, consistent with their measured incremental value (Figure 5). The rank ordering of predictor importance was highly concordant between KCHS and both NHANES cycles (Spearman ρ=0.97 pre, 0.99 post), indicating that the two populations use the predictors in a similar order; this suggests the transportability limit lies in the *strength*, not the *structure*, of predictor–outcome associations. The clearest exception was food insecurity, which ranked 11th in KCHS but 7th in NHANES pre, consistent with its different measurement (single item vs 10-item module) and much higher prevalence in the US.
docs/manuscript_17var.md:139:Subgroup discrimination in KCHS was fairly uniform (weighted AUROC 0.79–0.86): sex (female 0.82, male 0.83), education (0.81–0.83), and age (20–39 years 0.79 < 40–59 0.83 < ≥60 0.86). In NHANES, discrimination was lower and more variable across race/ethnicity: Mexican American 0.70, non-Hispanic White 0.78, non-Hispanic Black 0.76, non-Hispanic Asian 0.81, other Hispanic 0.78, other/multiracial 0.77 (small subgroup sizes, wide CIs), indicating reduced performance in some minority groups that should be reported as an equity caveat (Figure 6). Internal subgroup calibration was excellent (calibration slope 0.97–1.01 across sex and age strata).
docs/manuscript_17var.md:141:On decision-curve analysis (weighted, cohort-specific treat-all reference), the model provided positive net benefit across a clinically relevant threshold-probability range in all three datasets (Figure 7). At the Youden-optimal threshold, sensitivity/specificity were 0.75/0.76 (internal), 0.76/0.65 (NHANES pre), and 0.71/0.68 (NHANES post); positive predictive value was low (0.10–0.24) and negative predictive value high (0.95–0.99), as expected for population screening at low prevalence (full operating characteristics across thresholds in Multimedia Appendix 3, Table S1). Tuned to a triage-appropriate 85% sensitivity, the model flagged roughly half of the external population (pre 51%, post 55%) at a PPV of 0.14 (pre) and 0.19 (post)—a number needed to screen of 7.1 and 5.4—and the 8-item deployment model performed comparably (PPV 0.13/0.18; number needed to screen 7.6/5.6). After local intercept/slope recalibration, net benefit remained positive and exceeded both default strategies—screening everyone and screening no one—at threshold probabilities of 0.10–0.15 in every dataset; screen-all net benefit was in fact negative at these thresholds everywhere except NHANES post at 0.10, where the model still added +0.033 (Multimedia Appendix 3, Tables S4–S5). Clinical utility remains provisional pending prospective evaluation.
docs/manuscript_17var.md:166:**Table 1. Harmonized 17-predictor set (KCHS ↔ NHANES).** Predictors 1–14 are the protocol-specified set; 15–17 are the amendment additions (§2.5.1).
docs/manuscript_17var.md:190:The full year-specific harmonization crosswalk is provided as Multimedia Appendix 2.
docs/manuscript_17var.md:192:**Figure legends.** Figure 1: Weighted sample characteristics across cohorts—panel A, prevalence/proportion; panel B, means (exact values in Multimedia Appendix 3, Table S6). Figure 2: Calibration plots (internal, NHANES pre, NHANES post; original probabilities). Figure 3: Receiver operating characteristic curves (weighted) for internal, NHANES pre, and NHANES post. Figure 4: Sensitivity analyses—weighted AUROC across specifications for internal, NHANES pre, and NHANES post (exact values in Multimedia Appendix 3, Table S7). Figure 5: Global SHAP importance of the 17 predictors (KCHS). Figure 6: Subgroup discrimination (weighted AUROC) by sex, age, and education (KCHS) and race/ethnicity (NHANES pre). Figure 7: Decision curves (net benefit) for the three datasets. Tables: Table 1 (harmonized predictor set) and Table 2 (discrimination and calibration).
docs/manuscript_17var.md:201:NHANES data are openly available from the NCHS (https://wwwn.cdc.gov/nchs/nhanes/). KCHS microdata are access-controlled and were obtained through the KDCA raw-data request-and-review process (https://chs.kdca.go.kr); under the data-use agreement they cannot be redistributed. To support reproducibility, the analysis code, the variable-harmonization dictionary, the model card, and the protocol/pre-specification/amendment documents will be deposited in a public repository (DOI [to be inserted]) on acceptance; derived result tables are provided as Multimedia Appendices.
docs/manuscript_17var.md:212:## Multimedia Appendices
docs/manuscript_17var.md:213:- **Multimedia Appendix 1.** TRIPOD+AI reporting checklist.
docs/manuscript_17var.md:214:- **Multimedia Appendix 2.** Year-specific KCHS↔NHANES variable-harmonization crosswalk and predictor-set amendment/pre-specification document.
docs/manuscript_17var.md:215:- **Multimedia Appendix 3.** Clinical utility, characteristics, and sensitivity analyses: reference-threshold operating characteristics (Table S1), triage-oriented high-sensitivity operating points (Table S4), decision-curve net benefit (Table S5), weighted sample characteristics (Table S6), full sensitivity-analysis table (Table S7), and the parsimonious-model item-count trajectory (Table S8).
docs/manuscript_17var.md:216:- **Multimedia Appendix 4.** Transportability-ceiling analyses: leakage-controlled optimization attempts and deep tabular learners (Tables S2–S3) and the NHANES in-domain oracle by predictor set.

