===== docs/appendix2_crosswalk.md =====
   1: # Multimedia Appendix 2. Year-specific KCHSЁъNHANES harmonization crosswalk and the locked predictor-set amendment
   2: 
   3: Supports Methods Ёз2.5 and Ёз2.5.1. Section A documents how each harmonized variable was resolved in every KCHS survey year and in each NHANES cycle. Section B reproduces, in English, the predictor-set amendment that was written and locked before any model was fitted; the original Korean document (dated 2026-07-15) is included unchanged in the code release.
   4: 
   5: ---
   6: 
   7: ## A. Harmonization crosswalk
   8: 
   9: ### A.1 Outcome
  10: 
  11: | Concept | KCHS | NHANES pre (`P_`) | NHANES post (`_L`) | Harmonized definition |
  12: |---|---|---|---|---|
  13: | Current depression | `mtb_07a1`?`mtb_07i1` (9 items) | `DPQ010`?`DPQ090` | `DPQ010`?`DPQ090` | Nine items scored 0?3, summed; ЁУ10 = case. KCHS response codes 1?4 recoded to 0?3. Respondents missing any item excluded. |
  14: 
  15: ### A.2 The 17 harmonized predictors
  16: 
  17: KCHS variable names are not stable across survey years; the pipeline resolves each concept per year, first by variable name and then by a label regular expression, and writes the resolved name to a crosswalk log (`results/qc_crosswalk.txt`). Predictors 15?17 are the amendment additions.
  18: 
  19: | # | Concept | KCHS variable(s), by year | NHANES pre | NHANES post | Harmonized definition |
  20: |---|---|---|---|---|---|
  21: | 1 | Age | `age` (all years) | `RIDAGEYR` | `RIDAGEYR` | Continuous; analytic samples restricted to ЁУ20 y |
  22: | 2 | Sex | `sex` (all years) | `RIAGENDR` | `RIAGENDR` | Male / female |
  23: | 3 | Education | `sob_01z1` (all years) | `DMDEDUC2` | `DMDEDUC2` | 4 levels (ЁТelementary, middle, high, college+) |
  24: | 4 | Household income | `fma_24z1` (2017); `fma_14z1` (2018?2025) | `INDFMPIR` | `INDFMPIR` | Within-survey quintiles (rank-based, so the differing instruments are comparable in relative terms) |
  25: | 5 | Self-rated health | `qoa_01z1` (all years) | `HUQ010` | `HUQ010` | 5-level ordinal, 1 best ? 5 worst |
  26: | 6 | Partnered | `sod_02z2` (2017?2019); `sod_02z3` (2020?2025) | `DMDMARTZ` | `DMDMARTZ` | Married/cohabiting vs not |
  27: | 7 | Living alone | `fma_01z1` (2017?2019); `mbhld_co` (2020?2025) | `DMDHHSIZ` (**absent from the pre-pandemic DEMO file**) | `DMDHHSIZ` | Household size = 1. Retained as missing in NHANES pre rather than imputed |
  28: | 8 | Smoking | `sma_03z2` (current) + `sma_01z1` (lifetime) | `SMQ020`, `SMQ040` | `SMQ020`, `SMQ040` | Never / former / current |
  29: | 9 | Alcohol | `dra_01z1` (+ `drb_01z3`) | `ALQ111`, `ALQ121`, `ALQ130` | same | Current drinker (conservative common definition) |
  30: | 10 | Physical activity | `pha_04z1`/`pha_05z1`/`pha_06z1` (vigorous), `pha_07z1`/`pha_08z1`/`pha_09z1` (moderate), `phb_01z1` (walking); `pha_07z1` not fielded in 2018 (activity derived from the remaining components) | `PAQ650`/`PAQ655`/`PAD660` (vigorous), `PAQ665`/`PAQ670`/`PAD675` (moderate) | `PAD790Q`/`PAD790U`/`PAD800` (moderate), `PAD810Q`/`PAD810U`/`PAD820` (vigorous) | Meets aerobic recommendation (yes/no). Instruments differ between NHANES cycles; the binary recommendation threshold is the conservative common definition |
  31: | 11 | Sleep duration | `mtc_01z1` (2017?2019); `mtc_17z1` (2020?2022, 2024, 2025); **not fielded in 2023** | `SLD012` | `SLD012` | Hours per night (continuous) |
  32: | 12 | BMI | `oba_bmi` when present (2018, 2020, 2023, 2024); otherwise derived from `oba_02z1` (height) and `oba_03z1` (weight); **neither available in 2019** | `BMXBMI` | `BMXBMI` | Continuous. Self-reported in KCHS, measured in NHANES |
  33: | 13 | Hypertension | `hya_04z1` (all years) | `BPQ020` | `BPQ020` | Physician-diagnosed, yes/no |
  34: | 14 | Diabetes | `dia_04z1` (all years) | `DIQ010` | `DIQ010` | Physician-diagnosed, yes/no |
  35: | **15** | **Employment** | `soa_01z1` (9/9 years) | `OCD150` (P_OCQ) | `OCD150` (OCQ_L) | Binary: KCHS 1 = employed; NHANES 1?2 (working, or has a job but not at work) = employed, 3?4 = not |
  36: | **16** | **Self-rated oral health** | `ora_01z1` (9/9 years) | `OHQ845` (P_OHQ) | `OHQ845` (OHQ_L) | 5-level ordinal, 1 best ? 5 worst (same construction as self-rated health) |
  37: | **17** | **Food insecurity** | `nue_01z1` (7/9 years; **not fielded 2020?2021**) | `FSDAD` (P_FSQ) | `FSDAD` (FSQ_L) | Binary: KCHS categories 3?4 (sometimes/often insufficient) = insecure; NHANES adult food-security categories 3?4 (low/very low) = insecure. Single item vs 10-item module ? the most weakly harmonized predictor |
  38: | ? | Race/ethnicity (fairness analysis only, never a predictor) | not collected | `RIDRETH3` | `RIDRETH3` | NHANES subgroup analysis only |
  39: 
  40: ### A.3 Complex-survey design variables
  41: 
  42: | Element | KCHS | NHANES pre | NHANES post |
  43: |---|---|---|---|
  44: | Weight | `wt` (2017?2018); `wt_p` (2019?2025) | `WTMECPRP` (P_DEMO) | `WTMEC2YR` (DEMO_L) |
  45: | Stratum | `kstrata` | `SDMVSTRA` | `SDMVSTRA` |
  46: | Primary sampling unit | `jijum_cd` (2017?2018); `spot_no` (2019?2021); `SPOT_NO` (2022?2025) | `SDMVPSU` | `SDMVPSU` |
  47: | Design scale | 5,729 strata; 197,117 stratum?PSU combinations | 24 strata; 49 PSUs | 15 strata; 30 PSUs |
  48: 
  49: ### A.4 Analytic sample derivation
  50: 
  51: | Cohort | Adults ЁУ20 y | With complete PHQ-9 | Unweighted prevalence | Weighted prevalence |
  52: |---|---|---|---|---|
  53: | KCHS 2017?2025 | 2,071,211 | **2,048,510** | 3.607% | 3.58% |
  54: | NHANES pre-pandemic | 15,560 (all ages) Ёц 9,232 adults ЁУ20 y | **7,888** | 9.229% | 8.45% |
  55: | NHANES post-pandemic | 11,933 (all ages) Ёц 7,809 adults ЁУ20 y | **5,210** | 12.687% | 12.20% |
  56: 
  57: Per-year KCHS counts and structural gaps are logged in `results/qc_crosswalk.txt`. Depression-module non-response in NHANES (three or more PHQ-9 items unanswered) was 14.3% pre and 32.7% post and was differential by education and race/ethnicity; see Results Ёз3.1 and Limitations Ёз4.4.
  58: 
  59: ### A.5 Variables excluded as circular with the outcome
  60: 
  61: Pre-specified for exclusion because they re-measure the outcome construct rather than predict it:
  62: 
  63: | Excluded | Reason |
  64: |---|---|
  65: | KCHS `mtb_07a1`?`mtb_07i1` | the PHQ-9 items themselves (the outcome) |
  66: | KCHS `mtb_01z1` (depressed-mood experience) | a subset of the PHQ-9 construct |
  67: | KCHS `mtb_02z1`, `mte_01z1` (mental-health consultation) | consequence/proxy of depression |
  68: | KCHS `mtd_01z1`?`mtd_06z1` (suicidal ideation, attempt, counselling) | overlaps PHQ-9 item 9 |
  69: | KCHS `qoc_05z1` (EQ-5D anxiety/depression) | directly measures depression |
  70: | KCHS `qoc_07z1` (happiness index) | overlapping affective construct |
  71: | KCHS `mta_01z1` (perceived stress) | outcome-proximal; also 100% missing in NHANES |
  72: | NHANES `HUQ090` (saw a mental-health professional) | consequence/proxy; excluded on the external side as well |
  73: 
  74: ---
  75: 
  76: ## B. Predictor-set amendment (English rendition of the locked document)
  77: 
  78: **Status: locked before analysis ? written 2026-07-15, before any 17-predictor model was fitted.** The original states that the document is not to be revised after seeing results.
  79: 
  80: ### B.1 Rationale
  81: 
  82: The original protocol pre-specified 14 harmonized predictors (protocol Table 1). The supervising investigator approved a re-selection of predictors; the investigators elected to keep the external-validation design unchanged (develop in KCHS, apply to NHANES without refitting) and to expand only the set of variables that both surveys share. Sections Ёз2.4 (outcome definition), Ёз2.8 (model development) and Ёз2.10 (external-validation strategy) of the protocol were therefore left unchanged; only the predictor list was amended.
  83: 
  84: ### B.2 Eligibility criteria
  85: 
  86: Candidates were drawn from a crosswalk of all 616 KCHS concepts against the NHANES codebook for both cycles (a 175-variable extract of candidate items at the time of the amendment; a later, exhaustive extraction identified 265 variables common to both cycles). A candidate was eligible only if it was (i) available in both NHANES cycles, (ii) collected in more than one KCHS survey year, and (iii) not circular with the PHQ-9 outcome. These criteria use survey documentation and variable availability only; no NHANES outcome data were consulted.
  87: 
  88: ### B.3 Predictors added
  89: 
  90: Employment (`soa_01z1` Ёъ `OCD150`), self-rated oral health (`ora_01z1` Ёъ `OHQ845`), and household food insecurity (`nue_01z1` Ёъ `FSDAD`) were the three concepts adopted. Harmonized definitions are given in Table A.2.
  91: 
  92: Candidates rejected at this stage, with reasons: additional chronic conditions in KCHS (dyslipidemia, osteoporosis, cataract, arthritis) are fielded in a single year only; stroke and myocardial infarction are recorded in KCHS as *awareness of early warning signs* rather than physician diagnosis, so they do not match the NHANES concept; functional limitation exists only in the NHANES post-pandemic cycle.
  93: 
  94: ### B.4 Analysis plan (unchanged from the protocol)
  95: 
  96: Outcome PHQ-9 ЁУ10 in both surveys, with ЁУ5 as a sensitivity analysis. Development in KCHS 2017?2025 with three algorithms (elastic net, random forest, LightGBM); the primary model selected on internal cross-validation only, by CV-AUROC, then |calibration slope ? 1|, then parsimony; external performance is never used to choose a model. Preprocessing fitted in the development data and applied unchanged externally. Complex-survey?weighted evaluation with cluster bootstrap CIs. The KCHS-trained model applied to NHANES without refitting, evaluated separately in the pre- and post-pandemic cycles, with intercept/slope recalibration. The 17-predictor and the original 14-predictor models are reported side by side, internally and externally, so that the net effect of the expansion is visible.
  97: 
  98: ### B.5 Expectations declared in advance
  99: 
 100: The three additions were expected to be moderate-strength predictors, raising internal AUROC only slightly (roughly 0.824 Ёц 0.83). **External AUROC of 0.80 was explicitly not expected**, because earlier work had already localized the external gap to Korea?US concept shift rather than to a shortage of predictors. The document states that if the expansion does not improve external performance, that result is to be reported as it stands, since an absence of improvement is itself evidence that the gap is a transportability problem rather than a predictor-count problem.
 101: 
 102: ### B.6 Deviation log
 103: 
 104: One deviation from the locked document is reported. After the primary analysis was complete, the remaining harmonizable-but-unused items (public-assistance receipt, alcohol frequency and quantity, binge drinking, weekend sleep duration) were screened by comparing the direction of their adjusted associations with depression across the three cohorts. This screening inspected NHANES outcome associations and so lies outside the locked plan; it is reported in Methods Ёз2.5.1 as a post-hoc diagnostic. It did not change the predictor set: no item was added, because those with a consistent direction added no external discrimination (ЅФAUROC <0.001) and the remainder reversed direction between countries.
===== docs/appendix3_utility.md =====
   1: # Multimedia Appendix 3. Clinical utility, sample characteristics, and sensitivity analyses
   2: 
   3: All estimates complex-survey weighted. Supports Results Ёз3.1 (participant characteristics, Table S6), Ёз3.6 (parsimonious deployment model, Table S8), Ёз3.7 (sensitivity analyses, Table S7), and Ёз3.8 (interpretability, fairness, and clinical utility; Tables S1, S4?S5). The model is positioned as a screening/triage aid, not a diagnostic instrument.
   4: 
   5: ## Table S1. Operating characteristics at reference thresholds
   6: 
   7: | Set | Threshold | Sensitivity | Specificity | PPV | NPV |
   8: |---|---|---|---|---|---|
   9: | Internal | prevalence (0.036) | 0.706 | 0.797 | 0.115 | 0.986 |
  10: | Internal | Youden | 0.748 | 0.756 | 0.103 | 0.988 |
  11: | Internal | fixed 0.10 | 0.441 | 0.939 | 0.213 | 0.978 |
  12: | NHANES pre | prevalence (0.084) | 0.529 | 0.841 | 0.234 | 0.951 |
  13: | NHANES pre | Youden | 0.762 | 0.651 | 0.168 | 0.967 |
  14: | NHANES pre | fixed 0.10 | 0.485 | 0.864 | 0.248 | 0.948 |
  15: | NHANES post | prevalence (0.122) | 0.407 | 0.912 | 0.392 | 0.917 |
  16: | NHANES post | Youden | 0.714 | 0.678 | 0.236 | 0.945 |
  17: | NHANES post | fixed 0.10 | 0.448 | 0.890 | 0.361 | 0.921 |
  18: 
  19: ## Table S4. Triage-oriented high-sensitivity operating points
  20: 
  21: A screening/triage tool is typically tuned to high sensitivity. At sensitivity fixed to 0.85 and 0.90, the table gives the resulting specificity, positive predictive value (PPV), negative predictive value (NPV), the share of the population flagged (flag rate), and the number needed to screen to identify one case (NNS = 1/PPV). At these fixed-sensitivity operating points the 8-item deployment model performs comparably to the full 17-predictor model, even though its post-pandemic *discrimination* is 0.016 AUROC lower (Ёз3.6).
  22: 
  23: | Model | Set | Target | Specificity | PPV | NPV | Flag rate | NNS |
  24: |---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|
  25: | 17-predictor | Internal | sens 0.85 | 0.616 | 0.076 | 0.991 | 0.400 | 13.1 |
  26: | 17-predictor | Internal | sens 0.90 | 0.505 | 0.064 | 0.993 | 0.509 | 15.7 |
  27: | 17-predictor | NHANES pre | sens 0.85 | 0.524 | 0.142 | 0.974 | 0.507 | 7.1 |
  28: | 17-predictor | NHANES pre | sens 0.90 | 0.394 | 0.121 | 0.977 | 0.631 | 8.3 |
  29: | 17-predictor | NHANES post | sens 0.85 | 0.486 | 0.187 | 0.959 | 0.555 | 5.4 |
  30: | 17-predictor | NHANES post | sens 0.90 | 0.385 | 0.169 | 0.965 | 0.649 | 5.9 |
  31: | 8-item | Internal | sens 0.85 | 0.585 | 0.071 | 0.991 | 0.431 | 14.1 |
  32: | 8-item | NHANES pre | sens 0.85 | 0.481 | 0.131 | 0.972 | 0.547 | 7.6 |
