docs\model_card_17var.md:36:- Public, de-identified secondary data; no re-identification. Low PPV means many screen-positives are not depressed — must be paired with confirmatory assessment and support pathways, not automated action. Subgroup performance differences (age, race/ethnicity) require monitoring to avoid inequitable triage.
docs\manuscript_17var.md:17:**Methods.** Penalized logistic regression, random forest, and gradient boosting were developed in KCHS (2017–2025; pooled n=2,048,510; outcome-stratified 500,000-record training set and 100,000-record hold-out) using a harmonized 17-predictor set shared with NHANES—14 protocol-specified predictors plus employment, oral health, and food insecurity added by a documented amendment locked before analysis. All algorithm, feature-representation and hyperparameter selection used KCHS-internal cross-validation only; the trained model was applied to NHANES without refitting. Evaluation was complex-survey–weighted with stratified cluster bootstrap CIs, plus SHAP interpretation and subgroup fairness. Temporal validation contrasted the NCHS-defined pre-pandemic (2017–March 2020; n=7,888) and post-pandemic (2021–2023; n=5,210) cycles, with intercept/slope recalibration.
docs\manuscript_17var.md:81:SHAP global importance [9] and cross-survey rank concordance of predictor importance (Spearman correlation between KCHS and each NHANES cycle) as a mechanistic view of transportability; fairness by weighted subgroup AUROC and calibration slope across sex, age, education, and (NHANES) race/ethnicity; and a parsimonious model for a low-burden self-administered form, formed by entering predictors in descending order of global SHAP importance and refitting at each item count. The deployment item count was chosen on internal discrimination and item burden alone, with external performance reported afterwards rather than used for selection (§3.6). Positioned as a screening/triage aid, not a diagnostic.
docs\manuscript_17var.md:87:An adversarial audit corrected three silent data-processing defects before final analysis: (1) living-alone fabricated to a constant in NHANES pre (DMDHHSIZ absent → constant imputation) — corrected to preserve missingness; (2) NHANES post physical-activity coded 0 for all respondents due to string-typed unit fields mis-parsed to NaN — corrected with explicit unit mapping; (3) an income refusal code (77777) not treated as missing, placing non-respondents in the top income quintile — corrected. These affected data integrity but not the substantive conclusions.
docs\manuscript_17var.md:97:Weighted depression prevalence (PHQ-9 ≥10): KCHS 3.6%, NHANES pre 8.4%, post 12.2% (Figure 1). The surveys differed markedly on measured covariates—e.g., mean BMI 23.7 (KCHS) vs 29.8 (NHANES), and weighted food-insecurity prevalence 2.9% vs ~16%—consistent with genuinely distinct populations and, for food insecurity, with the single-item-vs-module measurement difference. DPQ item non-response was substantial in both NHANES cycles and much larger after the pandemic: among adults ≥20 years, 14.3% of the pre-pandemic and 32.7% of the post-pandemic respondents left three or more PHQ-9 items unanswered, yielding the complete-case analytic samples of 7,888 and 5,210. Non-response was differential—higher among respondents with less than high-school education (20.6% pre, 47.3% post) and among Asian and Black respondents—so the external cohorts, and particularly the post-pandemic fairness estimates, should be read as complete-case results. Reassuringly, adding respondents missing only one or two items using their person-mean changed weighted prevalence by ≤0.1 percentage points in both cycles. Exact values are tabulated in Multimedia Appendix 3, Table S6.
docs\manuscript_17var.md:107:| Metric | Internal | NHANES pre | pre (recal.) | NHANES post | post (recal.) |
docs\manuscript_17var.md:134:Discrimination was stable across specifications that alter modeling choices rather than the target condition (Figure 4): weighted training (0.831/0.773/0.764 internal/pre/post), missing-indicator imputation (0.833/0.770/0.767), multiple imputation by chained equations (0.833/0.770/0.767), and the 16- and 14-predictor variants (§3.4) all lay within 0.01 of the primary model internally. Restricting both training and evaluation to respondents aged ≥30 years *improved* discrimination (0.840/0.784/0.788), consistent with the lower subgroup performance observed at ages 20–39 (§3.8). Changing the outcome definition to PHQ-9 ≥5 lowered discrimination throughout (0.759/0.736/0.739) at a much higher weighted prevalence (16.1%/25.0%/32.1%), as expected when the target shifts from moderate-or-worse symptoms to any symptom burden; notably, the Korea–US gap almost disappears at this cut-off (0.759 vs 0.736–0.739), indicating that the transportability loss is concentrated in the more severe end of the symptom distribution. Data-integrity fixes (§2.13) did not alter substantive conclusions. Exact values are tabulated in Multimedia Appendix 3, Table S7.
docs\manuscript_17var.md:139:Subgroup discrimination in KCHS was fairly uniform (weighted AUROC 0.79–0.86): sex (female 0.82, male 0.83), education (0.81–0.83), and age (20–39 years 0.79 < 40–59 0.83 < ≥60 0.86). In NHANES, discrimination was lower and more variable across race/ethnicity: Mexican American 0.70, non-Hispanic White 0.78, non-Hispanic Black 0.76, non-Hispanic Asian 0.81, other Hispanic 0.78, other/multiracial 0.77 (small subgroup sizes, wide CIs), indicating reduced performance in some minority groups that should be reported as an equity caveat (Figure 6). Internal subgroup calibration was excellent (calibration slope 0.97–1.01 across sex and age strata).
docs\manuscript_17var.md:141:On decision-curve analysis (weighted, cohort-specific treat-all reference), the model provided positive net benefit across a clinically relevant threshold-probability range in all three datasets (Figure 7). At the Youden-optimal threshold, sensitivity/specificity were 0.75/0.76 (internal), 0.76/0.65 (NHANES pre), and 0.71/0.68 (NHANES post); positive predictive value was low (0.10–0.24) and negative predictive value high (0.95–0.99), as expected for population screening at low prevalence (full operating characteristics across thresholds in Multimedia Appendix 3, Table S1). Tuned to a triage-appropriate 85% sensitivity, the model flagged roughly half of the external population (pre 51%, post 55%) at a PPV of 0.14 (pre) and 0.19 (post)—a number needed to screen of 7.1 and 5.4—and the 8-item deployment model performed comparably (PPV 0.13/0.18; number needed to screen 7.6/5.6). After local intercept/slope recalibration, net benefit remained positive and exceeded both default strategies—screening everyone and screening no one—at threshold probabilities of 0.10–0.15 in every dataset; screen-all net benefit was in fact negative at these thresholds everywhere except NHANES post at 0.10, where the model still added +0.033 (Multimedia Appendix 3, Tables S4–S5). Clinical utility remains provisional pending prospective evaluation.
docs\manuscript_17var.md:154:Identical PHQ-9 outcome; genuinely external validation across country, health system, race/ethnicity, and survey mode; temporal validation with recalibration; complex-survey–weighted evaluation with stratified cluster CIs; a predictor-set amendment documented and locked before analysis, with the added predictors chosen on survey availability and non-circularity rather than on any outcome association; equal-effort tuning of the three candidate algorithms and of the 14-predictor comparator; a data-integrity audit; and leakage-controlled optimization that rules out overfitting-driven optimism.
docs\manuscript_17var.md:157:Cross-sectional (screens current symptoms, not future onset); differing measurement of alcohol, physical activity, income, BMI, and food insecurity; DPQ non-response of 14.3% (pre) and 32.7% (post) handled as complete-case and differential by education and race/ethnicity (§3.1), which may bias the external estimates—especially the post-pandemic subgroup results—in an unknown direction; living-alone unavailable in NHANES pre; food insecurity single-item and unmeasured in KCHS 2020–2021; reduced discrimination in some racial/ethnic subgroups (wide CIs); low PPV at population prevalence (triage aid, not diagnostic); and prior depression diagnosis not recorded in KCHS 2017–2025 (precluding a diagnosis-exclusion sensitivity analysis). Model development used a 500,000-record training set and a 100,000-record hold-out drawn from the 2,048,510 pooled records rather than the full pooled sample (§2.2); a training-set size experiment showed this did not cost external discrimination (§3.5), but internal estimates are hold-out rather than fully cross-validated. The 14-predictor comparator was trained with the same algorithm and a separately tuned configuration (§3.4), yet both models share a single preprocessing and evaluation pipeline, so the 14→17 comparison isolates predictor content rather than modeling strategy. Finally, the 8-item deployment model lost 0.016 AUROC relative to the full model in the post-pandemic cohort (§3.6), so item reduction should be re-evaluated whenever the deployment population differs materially from the development population.
