.\docs\appendix4_ceiling.md:81:- The 17-predictor external ceiling (~0.77) is a **concept-shift** limit: the transported model is already oracle-optimal for these predictors, and covariate-shift correction does not help (it lowers AUROC), indicating the predictorâ€“outcome relationship, not the covariate distribution, differs across populations.
.\docs\appendix4_ceiling.md:82:- Multimorbidity and healthcare utilization carry additional, transportable signal in NHANES (oracle 0.765â†’0.778). These domains are **not collected by KCHS** (only hypertension and diabetes are recorded as physician-diagnosed conditions across all survey years, both already included), so they cannot enter a KCHS-developed transportable model. Part of the residual ceiling is therefore development-survey coverage, not concept shift alone.
.\docs\cover_letter.md:11:**Why it matters for JMIR's readership.** Most published survey-based depression models report internal validation only and, in Korean data, achieve internal AUROCs up to ~0.90 while relying on predictors (e.g., perceived stress, depressed-mood items) that are near-tautological with the outcome. Our study instead asks the question that matters for any deployable digital screener: does it transport? We find that discrimination remains clinically useful but lower on external data (AUROC ~0.77), that calibration drifts but is fully recoverable by lightweight intercept/slope recalibration, andâ€”criticallyâ€”that this ~0.77 external ceiling is a *concept-shift* limit intrinsic to the transportable predictor set, not a modeling failure. We establish this rigorously with an in-domain oracle (fitting NHANES on its own labels caps at ~0.77, whereas the same predictors reach ~0.83 within Korea) and >450 leakage-controlled optimization experiments, and we show that predictor-importance rank order is highly concordant across surveys (Spearman Ïâ‰ˆ0.97â€“0.99). A parsimonious 8-item version retains near-full external discrimination, supporting a low-burden web/mobile triage toolâ€”squarely within JMIR's scope of internet-based health services.
.\docs\evidence_ledger.md:6:**Audit re-runs (2026-07-29):** `41_audit_recompute.py` (CITL definition, stratified cluster bootstrap, cut-off â‰¥5 external, age â‰¥30, SHAP-ordered parsimonious), `42_audit_tuning.py` (equal-protocol algorithm tuning, independently tuned 14-predictor comparator), `43_consolidate.py` (canonical result tables). The primary model reproduced exactly (internal 0.8315 / pre 0.7718 / post 0.7688).
