.\docs\model_card_17var.md:40:- **Cross-national ceiling ~0.77**: external discrimination is bounded by concept shift (predictorâ€“outcome relationships differ between Korea and the US); this is intrinsic to the transportable predictor set, confirmed by an in-domain oracle (~0.77) versus within-Korea performance (~0.83) and by >450 leakage-controlled optimization experiments.
.\docs\manuscript_17var.md:19:**Results.** Gradient boosting (LightGBM) was the primary model. Internal AUROC was 0.832 (95% CI 0.822â€“0.841), meeting the â‰¥0.80 target (H1). External discrimination remained clinically useful but lower (pre 0.772, 95% CI 0.745â€“0.797; post 0.769, 0.747â€“0.789); calibration deteriorated (slope 0.66â€“0.68; calibration-in-the-large +0.49 and +1.11, ie, systematic under-prediction) and was fully restored by recalibration (slopeâ†’0.99, CITLâ†’0.00; H2, H3 supported). Against an independently tuned 14-predictor model, the three added predictors improved external discrimination (pre 0.749â†’0.772, Î”+0.023; post 0.753â†’0.769, Î”+0.016; both *P*<.001). An 8-item model retained near-full external discrimination pre-pandemic (0.770 vs 0.772) but lost 0.016 post-pandemic (0.753 vs 0.769), partially supporting H4. Extensive leakage-controlled optimization (>450 experiments) did not exceed ~0.77 externally; an in-domain oracle (fitting NHANES on its own labels) capped at ~0.77, whereas the same predictors reached 0.81â€“0.82 in Korean samples of the same size (0.83 at full scale), indicating concept shift rather than sample size or model choice.
.\docs\manuscript_17var.md:128:We localized the ceiling with an **in-domain oracle**: fitting NHANES on its own labels (a deliberate Â§2.10 violation, for diagnosis only) under cross-validation. Across logistic, penalized-logistic, histogram-gradient-boosting and LightGBM learners, the honest 5-fold cross-validated oracle reached only 0.765â€“0.772 (best learner 0.772, 95% CI 0.757â€“0.787 by stratified cluster bootstrap). The KCHS-transferred model (pre 0.772, post 0.769) therefore already **sits within the maximum discrimination extractable from these predictors within NHANES itself**â€”the transported model is at, not below, its achievable ceiling. Matching sample size does not close the gap: reducing the Korean data to the NHANES sample size and cross-validating within it still yielded 0.823 at n=7,888 (the pre-pandemic cohort size) and 0.810 at n=13,098 (the pooled oracle size), rising to 0.830 only at n=200,000 (best of three learners, 5-fold weighted AUROC). At identical n, in other words, the same 17 predictors carry 0.81â€“0.82 of discrimination in Korea against an NHANES ceiling of ~0.77. Three further checks converged on the same conclusion: (i) covariate-shift correction by density-ratio reweighting of the KCHS training sample toward the NHANES covariate distribution (labels unused) *reduced* external AUROC (to 0.766/0.758), indicating the gap is not covariate shift; (ii) increasing the KCHS training set from 0.5M to 2M examples did not raiseâ€”and slightly loweredâ€”external discrimination, consistent with source-specific overfitting rather than an estimation-limited ceiling; and (iii) adding every remaining harmonized-but-unused survey item (public-assistance receipt, alcohol-consumption detail, weekend sleep) left external AUROC unchanged (Î”<0.001). Deep tabular learners (a multilayer perceptron, TabNet, and an FT-Transformer) were additionally trained and transferred; all fell below gradient boosting internally and none exceeded it on both external cohorts (Multimedia Appendix 4), consistent with the model-agnostic ceiling. The residual gap thus reflects **concept shift** (predictorâ€“outcome relationships differ between populations), not sample size, model choice, covariate imbalance, or omitted harmonized information, and is not closable by recalibration (which corrects calibration, not rank ordering).
.\docs\manuscript_17var.md:131:Predictors were entered in descending order of global SHAP importance and the model refitted at each item count (internal / external-pre / external-post): 3 items 0.782/0.713/0.726; 5 items 0.809/0.741/0.745; 6 items 0.815/0.752/0.754; 7 items 0.819/0.761/0.758; **8 items 0.825/0.770/0.753**; 12 items 0.831/0.771/0.767; 17 items 0.832/0.772/0.769. Eight items was the smallest count whose internal discrimination came within 0.01 AUROC of the full model (0.825 vs 0.832; 7 items fell short at 0.819), and this criterion used internal data only. The resulting 8-item model (self-rated health, age, sex, sleep duration, oral health, partnership, employment, smoking) retained essentially all external discrimination in the pre-pandemic cohort (0.770 vs 0.772) but lost 0.016 in the post-pandemic cohort (0.753 vs 0.769); **H4 is therefore supported for the pre-pandemic cohort and only partially for the post-pandemic cohort**, where a 12-item version recovers the difference (0.767). The 8-item version remains suitable for a low-burden self-administered form, with the post-pandemic shortfall reported as a deployment caveat. The full item-count trajectory is tabulated in Multimedia Appendix 3, Table S8.
.\docs\manuscript_17var.md:139:Subgroup discrimination in KCHS was fairly uniform (weighted AUROC 0.785â€“0.859): sex (female 0.822, 95% CI 0.811â€“0.833; male 0.831, 0.816â€“0.845), education (0.813â€“0.830), and age (20â€“39 years 0.785, 0.766â€“0.802 < 40â€“59 years 0.828, 0.812â€“0.843 < â‰¥60 years 0.859, 0.849â€“0.870). In the NHANES pre-pandemic cohort, discrimination was lower and far less precisely estimated: Mexican American 0.697 (0.646â€“0.751; n=914), non-Hispanic Black 0.762 (0.738â€“0.786; n=2,096), other/multiracial 0.772 (0.698â€“0.840; n=381), other Hispanic 0.777 (0.747â€“0.808; n=813), non-Hispanic White 0.778 (0.743â€“0.809; n=2,798), and non-Hispanic Asian 0.810 (0.748â€“0.857; n=886). These intervals overlap one another and the cohort-level estimate, so the ordering should not be read as established differential performance; the lowest point estimate, in Mexican American respondents, nonetheless warrants monitoring as an equity caveat (Figure 6). Internal subgroup calibration was good (slope 0.97â€“1.02 across sex and age strata, 0.93â€“1.03 including education strata), whereas external subgroup calibration slopes were uniformly below 1 (0.58â€“0.78 across NHANES race/ethnicity groups), mirroring the cohort-level under-prediction that recalibration corrects (Â§3.3). Exact subgroup values with CIs are tabulated in Multimedia Appendix 3, Table S9.
.\docs\manuscript_17var.md:151:Prior ML depression models built on Korean national surveys report strong internal performance but no external validation. Using KNHANES 2014â€“2018 restricted to non-smokers at risk of second-hand smoke exposure (n=11,463), Kim et al [12] classified PHQ-9 â‰¥10 with internal AUROC up to 0.90; using the 2023 KCHS restricted to culturally diverse families (n=2,568), Kim et al [11] predicted the continuous PHQ-9 score with a test RÂ² of 33%â€”both identifying perceived stress and subjective health as dominant predictors, and neither attempting external validation. Two deliberate design choices explain why our internal discrimination is lower (0.83): we excluded outcome-proximal predictors such as perceived stress and single-item depressed-mood questions, which inflate apparent performance but are near-tautological with the outcome; and we restricted the predictor set to variables transportable to a second country. In exchange, this study provides what prior work did notâ€”genuine cross-national and cross-pandemic external validation, an explicit transportability ceiling characterized by an in-domain oracle and >450 leakage-controlled experiments, and a recalibration analysis. That the same predictors carry substantially more signal within Korea (~0.83) than in the US (~0.77) is a concrete instance of the dataset-shift problem described for deployed clinical AI [10], and underscores that high internally-reported AUROCs do not guarantee cross-population usefulness.
.\docs\manuscript_17var.md:159:The transportable predictor set is also bounded by the *development* survey's content. In a coverage analysis we extracted candidate domains available in NHANES but not harmonizable to KCHSâ€”physician-diagnosed multimorbidity (asthma, arthritis, thyroid, chronic obstructive pulmonary disease, liver disease, and cancer, excluding circulatory conditions) and healthcare utilizationâ€”and found that adding them raised the NHANES in-domain oracle from 0.765 to 0.778. KCHS, however, records only hypertension and diabetes among physician-diagnosed conditions (both already included) and does not collect a broader chronic-disease inventory, hospitalization, or visit counts; these domains therefore cannot enter a KCHS-developed transportable model. Part of the residual external gap is thus attributable to development-survey coverage rather than concept shift alone, and a development survey that captured multimorbidity and utilization could plausibly lift the transportable ceiling by ~0.01 AUROC.
.\docs\manuscript_17var.md:216:- **Multimedia Appendix 4.** Transportability-ceiling analyses: leakage-controlled optimization attempts and deep tabular learners (Tables S2â€“S3) and the NHANES in-domain oracle by predictor set.
