.\wf\codex_review.log:441:docs/manuscript_17var.md:128:We localized the ceiling with an **in-domain oracle**: fitting NHANES on its own labels (a deliberate Â§2.10 violation, for diagnosis only) under cross-validation. Across logistic, penalized-logistic, histogram- and tree-gradient-boosting learners, the honest 5-fold cross-validated oracle reached only ~0.77 (approximately 0.765â€“0.775 depending on learner; pooled bootstrap 95% CI â‰ˆ0.75â€“0.79), and even the optimistic in-sample (apparent) bound was 0.773. The KCHS-transferred model (pre 0.772, post 0.769) therefore already **sits within the maximum discrimination extractable from these predictors within NHANES itself**â€”the transported model is at, not below, its achievable ceiling. Matching sample size does not close the gap: reducing the Korean data to the NHANES sample size and cross-validating within it still yielded 0.823 at n=7,888 (the pre-pandemic cohort size) and 0.810 at n=13,098 (the pooled oracle size), rising to 0.830 only at n=200,000 (best of three learners, 5-fold weighted AUROC). At identical n, in other words, the same 17 predictors carry 0.81â€“0.82 of discrimination in Korea against an NHANES ceiling of ~0.77. Three further checks converged on the same conclusion: (i) covariate-shift correction by density-ratio reweighting of the KCHS training sample toward the NHANES covariate distribution (labels unused) *reduced* external AUROC (to 0.766/0.758), indicating the gap is not covariate shift; (ii) increasing the KCHS training set from 0.5M to 2M examples did not raiseâ€”and slightly loweredâ€”external discrimination, consistent with source-specific overfitting rather than an estimation-limited ceiling; and (iii) adding every remaining harmonized-but-unused survey item (public-assistance receipt, alcohol-consumption detail, weekend sleep) left external AUROC unchanged (Î”<0.001). Deep tabular learners (a multilayer perceptron, TabNet, and an FT-Transformer) were additionally trained and transferred; all fell below gradient boosting internally and none exceeded it on both external cohorts (Multimedia Appendix 4), consistent with the model-agnostic ceiling. The residual gap thus reflects **concept shift** (predictorâ€“outcome relationships differ between populations), not sample size, model choice, covariate imbalance, or omitted harmonized information, and is not closable by recalibration (which corrects calibration, not rank ordering).
.\wf\codex_review.log:442:docs/manuscript_17var.md:131:Predictors were entered in descending order of global SHAP importance and the model refitted at each item count (internal / external-pre / external-post): 3 items 0.782/0.713/0.726; 5 items 0.809/0.741/0.745; 6 items 0.815/0.752/0.754; 7 items 0.819/0.761/0.758; **8 items 0.825/0.770/0.753**; 12 items 0.831/0.771/0.767; 17 items 0.832/0.772/0.769. Eight items was the smallest count whose internal discrimination came within 0.01 AUROC of the full model (0.825 vs 0.832; 7 items fell short at 0.819), and this criterion used internal data only. The resulting 8-item model (self-rated health, age, sex, sleep duration, oral health, partnership, employment, smoking) retained essentially all external discrimination in the pre-pandemic cohort (0.770 vs 0.772) but lost 0.016 in the post-pandemic cohort (0.753 vs 0.769); **H4 is therefore supported for the pre-pandemic cohort and only partially for the post-pandemic cohort**, where a 12-item version recovers the difference (0.767). The 8-item version remains suitable for a low-burden self-administered form, with the post-pandemic shortfall reported as a deployment caveat. The full item-count trajectory is tabulated in Multimedia Appendix 3, Table S8.
.\wf\codex_review.log:455:docs/manuscript_17var.md:216:- **Multimedia Appendix 4.** Transportability-ceiling analyses: leakage-controlled optimization attempts and deep tabular learners (Tables S2â€“S3) and the NHANES in-domain oracle by predictor set.
.\wf\codex_review.log:478:  19: **Results.** Gradient boosting (LightGBM) was the primary model. Internal AUROC was 0.832 (95% CI 0.822?0.841), meeting the â‰¥0.80 target (H1). External discrimination remained clinically useful but lower (pre 0.772, 95% CI 0.745?0.797; post 0.769, 0.747?0.789); calibration deteriorated (slope 0.66?0.68; calibration-in-the-large +0.49 and +1.11, ie, systematic under-prediction) and was fully restored by recalibration (slopeâ†’0.99, CITLâ†’0.00; H2, H3 supported). Against an independently tuned 14-predictor model, the three added predictors improved external discrimination (pre 0.749â†’0.772, Î”+0.023; post 0.753â†’0.769, Î”+0.016; both *P*<.001). An 8-item model retained near-full external discrimination pre-pandemic (0.770 vs 0.772) but lost 0.016 post-pandemic (0.753 vs 0.769), partially supporting H4. Extensive leakage-controlled optimization (>450 experiments) did not exceed ~0.77 externally; an in-domain oracle (fitting NHANES on its own labels) capped at ~0.77, whereas the same predictors reached 0.81?0.82 in Korean samples of the same size (0.83 at full scale), indicating concept shift rather than sample size or model choice.
.\wf\codex_review.log:587: 128: We localized the ceiling with an **in-domain oracle**: fitting NHANES on its own labels (a deliberate Â§2.10 violation, for diagnosis only) under cross-validation. Across logistic, penalized-logistic, histogram- and tree-gradient-boosting learners, the honest 5-fold cross-validated oracle reached only ~0.77 (approximately 0.765?0.775 depending on learner; pooled bootstrap 95% CI ?0.75?0.79), and even the optimistic in-sample (apparent) bound was 0.773. The KCHS-transferred model (pre 0.772, post 0.769) therefore already **sits within the maximum discrimination extractable from these predictors within NHANES itself**?the transported model is at, not below, its achievable ceiling. Matching sample size does not close the gap: reducing the Korean data to the NHANES sample size and cross-validating within it still yielded 0.823 at n=7,888 (the pre-pandemic cohort size) and 0.810 at n=13,098 (the pooled oracle size), rising to 0.830 only at n=200,000 (best of three learners, 5-fold weighted AUROC). At identical n, in other words, the same 17 predictors carry 0.81?0.82 of discrimination in Korea against an NHANES ceiling of ~0.77. Three further checks converged on the same conclusion: (i) covariate-shift correction by density-ratio reweighting of the KCHS training sample toward the NHANES covariate distribution (labels unused) *reduced* external AUROC (to 0.766/0.758), indicating the gap is not covariate shift; (ii) increasing the KCHS training set from 0.5M to 2M examples did not raise?and slightly lowered?external discrimination, consistent with source-specific overfitting rather than an estimation-limited ceiling; and (iii) adding every remaining harmonized-but-unused survey item (public-assistance receipt, alcohol-consumption detail, weekend sleep) left external AUROC unchanged (Î”<0.001). Deep tabular learners (a multilayer perceptron, TabNet, and an FT-Transformer) were additionally trained and transferred; all fell below gradient boos