.\wf\codex_review.log:610: 151: Prior ML depression models built on Korean national surveys report strong internal performance but no external validation. Using KNHANES 2014?2018 restricted to non-smokers at risk of second-hand smoke exposure (n=11,463), Kim et al [12] classified PHQ-9 â‰¥10 with internal AUROC up to 0.90; using the 2023 KCHS restricted to culturally diverse families (n=2,568), Kim et al [11] predicted the continuous PHQ-9 score with a test RÂ² of 33%?both identifying perceived stress and subjective health as dominant predictors, and neither attempting external validation. Two deliberate design choices explain why our internal discrimination is lower (0.83): we excluded outcome-proximal predictors such as perceived stress and single-item depressed-mood questions, which inflate apparent performance but are near-tautological with the outcome; and we restricted the predictor set to variables transportable to a second country. In exchange, this study provides what prior work did not?genuine cross-national and cross-pandemic external validation, an explicit transportability ceiling characterized by an in-domain oracle and >450 leakage-controlled experiments, and a recalibration analysis. That the same predictors carry substantially more signal within Korea (~0.83) than in the US (~0.77) is a concrete instance of the dataset-shift problem described for deployed clinical AI [10], and underscores that high internally-reported AUROCs do not guarantee cross-population usefulness.
.\wf\codex_review.log:618: 159: The transportable predictor set is also bounded by the *development* survey's content. In a coverage analysis we extracted candidate domains available in NHANES but not harmonizable to KCHS?physician-diagnosed multimorbidity (asthma, arthritis, thyroid, chronic obstructive pulmonary disease, liver disease, and cancer, excluding circulatory conditions) and healthcare utilization?and found that adding them raised the NHANES in-domain oracle from 0.765 to 0.778. KCHS, however, records only hypertension and diabetes among physician-diagnosed conditions (both already included) and does not collect a broader chronic-disease inventory, hospitalization, or visit counts; these domains therefore cannot enter a KCHS-developed transportable model. Part of the residual external gap is thus attributable to development-survey coverage rather than concept shift alone, and a development survey that captured multimorbidity and utilization could plausibly lift the transportable ceiling by ~0.01 AUROC.
.\wf\codex_review.log:675: 216: - **Multimedia Appendix 4.** Transportability-ceiling analyses: leakage-controlled optimization attempts and deep tabular learners (Tables S2?S3) and the NHANES in-domain oracle by predictor set.
