.\wf\_docx_check.txt:10:Results. Gradient boosting (LightGBM) was the primary model. Internal AUROC was 0.832 (95% CI 0.822â€“0.841), meeting the â‰¥0.80 target (H1). External discrimination remained clinically useful but lower (pre 0.772, 95% CI 0.745â€“0.797; post 0.769, 0.747â€“0.789); calibration deteriorated (slope 0.66â€“0.68; calibration-in-the-large +0.49 and +1.11, ie, systematic under-prediction) and was fully restored by recalibration (slopeâ†’0.99, CITLâ†’0.00; H2, H3 supported). Against an independently tuned 14-predictor model, the three added predictors improved external discrimination (pre 0.749â†’0.772, Î”+0.023; post 0.753â†’0.769, Î”+0.016; both P&lt;.001). An 8-item model retained near-full external discrimination pre-pandemic (0.770 vs 0.772) but lost 0.016 post-pandemic (0.753 vs 0.769), partially supporting H4. Extensive leakage-controlled optimization (&gt;450 experiments) did not exceed ~0.77 externally; an in-domain oracle (fitting NHANES on its own labels) capped at ~0.77, whereas the same predictors reached ~0.83 within Korea, indicating concept shift rather than sample size or model choice.
.\wf\_docx_check.txt:120:We localized the ceiling with an in-domain oracle: fitting NHANES on its own labels (a deliberate Â§2.10 violation, for diagnosis only) under cross-validation. Across logistic, penalized-logistic, histogram- and tree-gradient-boosting learners, the honest 5-fold cross-validated oracle reached only ~0.77 (approximately 0.765â€“0.775 depending on learner; pooled bootstrap 95% CI â‰ˆ0.75â€“0.79), and even the optimistic in-sample (apparent) bound was 0.773. The KCHS-transferred model (pre 0.772, post 0.769) therefore already sits within the maximum discrimination extractable from these predictors within NHANES itselfâ€”the transported model is at, not below, its achievable ceilingâ€”whereas the same 17 predictors reached ~0.83 in a same-sized Korean sample. Three further pre-specified checks converged on the same conclusion: (i) covariate-shift correction by density-ratio reweighting of the KCHS training sample toward the NHANES covariate distribution (labels unused) reduced external AUROC (to 0.766/0.758), indicating the gap is not covariate shift; (ii) increasing the KCHS training set from 0.5M to 2M examples did not raiseâ€”and slightly loweredâ€”external discrimination, consistent with source-specific overfitting rather than an estimation-limited ceiling; and (iii) adding every remaining harmonized-but-unused survey item (public-assistance receipt, alcohol-consumption detail, weekend sleep) left external AUROC unchanged (Î”&lt;0.001). Deep tabular learners (a multilayer perceptron, TabNet, and an FT-Transformer) were additionally trained and transferred; all fell below gradient boosting internally and none exceeded it on both external cohorts (Multimedia Appendix 4), consistent with the model-agnostic ceiling. The residual gap thus reflects concept shift (predictorâ€“outcome relationships differ between populations), not sample size, model choice, covariate imbalance, or omitted harmonized information, and is not closable by recalibration (which corrects calibration, not rank ordering).
.\wf\_docx_check.txt:122:Predictors were entered in descending order of global SHAP importance and the model refitted at each item count (internal / external-pre / external-post): 3 items 0.782/0.713/0.726; 5 items 0.809/0.741/0.745; 6 items 0.815/0.752/0.754; 7 items 0.819/0.761/0.758; 8 items 0.825/0.770/0.753; 12 items 0.831/0.771/0.767; 17 items 0.832/0.772/0.769. Eight items was the smallest count whose internal discrimination came within 0.01 AUROC of the full model (0.825 vs 0.832; 7 items fell short at 0.819), and this criterion used internal data only. The resulting 8-item model (self-rated health, age, sex, sleep duration, oral health, partnership, employment, smoking) retained essentially all external discrimination in the pre-pandemic cohort (0.770 vs 0.772) but lost 0.016 in the post-pandemic cohort (0.753 vs 0.769); H4 is therefore supported for the pre-pandemic cohort and only partially for the post-pandemic cohort, where a 12-item version recovers the difference (0.767). The 8-item version remains suitable for a low-burden self-administered form, with the post-pandemic shortfall reported as a deployment caveat. The full item-count trajectory is tabulated in Multimedia Appendix 3, Table S8.
.\wf\_docx_check.txt:133:Prior ML depression models built on Korean national surveys report strong internal performance but no external validation. Using KNHANES, Kim et al [12] classified PHQ-9 â‰¥10 with internal AUROC up to 0.90; using the KCHS, Kim et al [11] predicted the continuous PHQ-9 score with RÂ²â‰ˆ0.33â€”both identifying perceived stress and subjective health as dominant predictors. Two deliberate design choices explain why our internal discrimination is lower (0.83): we excluded outcome-proximal predictors such as perceived stress and single-item depressed-mood questions, which inflate apparent performance but are near-tautological with the outcome; and we restricted the predictor set to variables transportable to a second country. In exchange, this study provides what prior work did notâ€”genuine cross-national and cross-pandemic external validation, an explicit transportability ceiling characterized by an in-domain oracle and &gt;450 leakage-controlled experiments, and a recalibration analysis. That the same predictors carry substantially more signal within Korea (~0.83) than in the US (~0.77) is a concrete instance of the dataset-shift problem described for deployed clinical AI [10], and underscores that high internally-reported AUROCs do not guarantee cross-population usefulness.
.\wf\_docx_check.txt:138:The transportable predictor set is also bounded by the development survey's content. In a coverage analysis we extracted candidate domains available in NHANES but not harmonizable to KCHSâ€”physician-diagnosed multimorbidity (asthma, arthritis, thyroid, chronic obstructive pulmonary disease, liver disease, and cancer, excluding circulatory conditions) and healthcare utilizationâ€”and found that adding them raised the NHANES in-domain oracle from 0.765 to 0.778. KCHS, however, records only hypertension and diabetes among physician-diagnosed conditions (both already included) and does not collect a broader chronic-disease inventory, hospitalization, or visit counts; these domains therefore cannot enter a KCHS-developed transportable model. Part of the residual external gap is thus attributable to development-survey coverage rather than concept shift alone, and a development survey that captured multimorbidity and utilization could plausibly lift the transportable ceiling by ~0.01 AUROC.
.\wf\_docx_check.txt:260:Multimedia Appendix 4. Transportability-ceiling analyses: leakage-controlled optimization attempts and deep tabular learners (Tables S2â€“S3) and the NHANES in-domain oracle by predictor set.

