docs/appendix2_crosswalk.md:96:Outcome PHQ-9 ≥10 in both surveys, with ≥5 as a sensitivity analysis. Development in KCHS 2017–2025 with three algorithms (elastic net, random forest, LightGBM); the primary model selected on internal cross-validation only, by CV-AUROC, then |calibration slope − 1|, then parsimony; external performance is never used to choose a model. Preprocessing fitted in the development data and applied unchanged externally. Complex-survey–weighted evaluation with cluster bootstrap CIs. The KCHS-trained model applied to NHANES without refitting, evaluated separately in the pre- and post-pandemic cycles, with intercept/slope recalibration. The 17-predictor and the original 14-predictor models are reported side by side, internally and externally, so that the net effect of the expansion is visible.
docs/appendix2_crosswalk.md:100:The three additions were expected to be moderate-strength predictors, raising internal AUROC only slightly (roughly 0.824 → 0.83). **External AUROC of 0.80 was explicitly not expected**, because earlier work had already localized the external gap to Korea–US concept shift rather than to a shortage of predictors. The document states that if the expansion does not improve external performance, that result is to be reported as it stands, since an absence of improvement is itself evidence that the gap is a transportability problem rather than a predictor-count problem.
docs/appendix4_ceiling.md:3:Supporting Results §3.5 (concept-shift ceiling) and Discussion §4.4 (development-survey coverage). All model selection used KCHS-internal criteria only; NHANES labels were used solely for the in-domain oracle diagnostic (a deliberate §2.10 violation, quarantined to this appendix). Weighted AUROC throughout.
