docs/appendix3_utility.md:111:Weighted AUROC with 95% CIs from the stratified cluster bootstrap (1000 replicates), and the weighted calibration slope, by subgroup. KCHS subgroups are evaluated in the internal hold-out; race/ethnicity is available only in NHANES and is used for fairness analysis only, never as a predictor. Intervals for the NHANES race/ethnicity groups are wide and mutually overlapping.
docs/manuscript_17var.md:19:**Results.** Gradient boosting (LightGBM) was the primary model. Internal AUROC was 0.832 (95% CI 0.822–0.841), meeting the ≥0.80 target (H1). External discrimination remained clinically useful but lower (pre 0.772, 95% CI 0.745–0.797; post 0.769, 0.747–0.789); calibration deteriorated (slope 0.66–0.68; calibration-in-the-large +0.49 and +1.11, ie, systematic under-prediction) and was fully restored by recalibration (slope→0.99, CITL→0.00; H2, H3 supported). Against an independently tuned 14-predictor model, the three added predictors improved external discrimination (pre 0.749→0.772, Δ+0.023; post 0.753→0.769, Δ+0.016; both *P*<.001). An 8-item model retained near-full external discrimination pre-pandemic (0.770 vs 0.772) but lost 0.016 post-pandemic (0.753 vs 0.769), partially supporting H4. Extensive leakage-controlled optimization (>450 experiments) did not exceed ~0.77 externally; an in-domain oracle (fitting NHANES on its own labels) capped at ~0.77, whereas the same predictors reached 0.81–0.82 in Korean samples of the same size (0.83 at full scale), indicating concept shift rather than sample size or model choice.
docs/manuscript_17var.md:23:**Keywords:** depression; mass screening; machine learning; supervised machine learning; models, statistical; external validation; transportability; concept shift; COVID-19; mobile health; Patient Health Questionnaire; Republic of Korea.
docs/manuscript_17var.md:44:Retrospective secondary-data study developing a clinical prediction model with external validation; reported per TRIPOD+AI (2024) [4], risk of bias self-assessed with PROBAST [5]. The study protocol and statistical analysis plan were written and dated before the analysis, and the predictor-set expansion described in §2.5 is a documented amendment that was likewise locked before any model was fitted (§2.5.1). Multimedia Appendix 2 provides the year-specific harmonization crosswalk together with an English rendition of the locked amendment; the unmodified original protocol, statistical analysis plan, and amendment document will be deposited with the analysis code, hyperparameter grids, and model card in a public repository (DOI [to be inserted]; see Data Availability).
docs/manuscript_17var.md:59:**2.5.1 Predictor-set amendment and selection provenance.** The three added predictors were identified by crosswalking all 616 KCHS concepts against the NHANES codebook for both cycles (a 175-variable extract of candidate items at the time of the amendment; a later, exhaustive extraction identified 265 variables common to both cycles). The amendment was written and locked before any model was fitted (Multimedia Appendix 2) and required a candidate to be (i) available in both NHANES cycles, (ii) collected in more than one KCHS survey year, and (iii) not circular with the PHQ-9 outcome. Employment, self-rated oral health, and household food insecurity were the three concepts adopted. Candidates rejected at this stage failed on single-year collection (e.g., dyslipidemia, osteoporosis, arthritis), concept mismatch (KCHS records only *awareness of early warning signs* for stroke and myocardial infarction, not physician diagnosis), or absence from the NHANES pre-pandemic cycle (functional limitation). Criteria (i)–(iii) use survey documentation and variable availability only; **no NHANES outcome data entered the amendment**. Each added predictor has an established association with depression in the epidemiological literature—food insecurity (pooled odds ratio ≈1.4) [13], unemployment (≈1.9–2.1) [14], and poor oral health (a bidirectional relationship) [15]—supporting biological and social plausibility rather than data-driven selection. Circular/outcome-contaminated variables (PHQ items, suicidal ideation, EQ-5D anxiety/depression, happiness, perceived stress, mental-health service use including NHANES HUQ090) were pre-specified for exclusion.
docs/manuscript_17var.md:69:Development is not power-limiting given KCHS size: the 500,000-record training set contains approximately 18,000 events (about 1,000 events per candidate predictor) and the 100,000-record hold-out approximately 3,600 events. NHANES event counts (728 pre, 661 post; weighted prevalence 8.4% and 12.2%) govern external calibration and decision-curve precision.
docs/manuscript_17var.md:72:Algorithms: penalized logistic regression (elastic net), random forest, and gradient boosting (LightGBM). Development proceeded in two selection steps, both confined to the KCHS training set. First, the gradient-boosting hyperparameters and the feature representation were chosen by a randomized search over 24 LightGBM configurations crossed with two representations—main effects only, versus main effects plus four pre-specified interaction terms (age×self-rated health, self-rated health×oral health, age×employment, income×employment)—evaluated by 4-fold survey-weighted cross-validation in a 300,000-record subsample of the training set; the main-effects-only representation won, and adding interaction terms did not improve cross-validated discrimination. Second, all three algorithms were tuned over comparable 5-configuration grids under an identical protocol (5-fold stratified cross-validation in a 200,000-record subsample of the training set) and compared at each algorithm's best configuration, so that no algorithm was advantaged by differential tuning effort. The primary model was selected a priori by cross-validated AUROC, then |calibration slope − 1|, then parsimony. **No NHANES data were used in any selection or tuning step.** Complex-survey weights were handled by unweighted training with weighted evaluation (probed in sensitivity). LightGBM was selected (best internal CV-AUROC 0.840 vs random forest 0.835 and elastic net 0.822) and refitted on the full 500,000-record training set; the selected hyperparameters and the full search grids are reported in the model card, which is deposited with the code (see Data Availability).
docs/manuscript_17var.md:75:Discrimination (AUROC, AUPRC), calibration, and operating characteristics, all complex-survey–weighted. Calibration was summarized by the calibration slope and by calibration-in-the-large (CITL), the latter estimated as the intercept of a weighted logistic model with the linear predictor entered as an offset (ie, with the slope fixed at 1), together with the Brier score and expected calibration error (ECE); a positive CITL indicates that observed risk exceeds predicted risk. Ninety-five percent CIs were obtained by a stratified cluster bootstrap resampling primary sampling units with replacement *within* design strata (1000 replicates) [16]. Clinical utility by decision-curve analysis [8].
docs/manuscript_17var.md:81:SHAP global importance [9] and cross-survey rank concordance of predictor importance (Spearman correlation between KCHS and each NHANES cycle) as a mechanistic view of transportability; fairness by weighted subgroup AUROC and calibration slope across sex, age, education, and (NHANES) race/ethnicity; and a parsimonious model for a low-burden self-administered form, formed by entering predictors in descending order of global SHAP importance and refitting at each item count. The deployment item count was chosen on internal discrimination and item burden alone, with external performance reported afterwards rather than used for selection (§3.6). Positioned as a screening/triage aid, not a diagnostic.
docs/manuscript_17var.md:97:Weighted depression prevalence (PHQ-9 ≥10): KCHS 3.6%, NHANES pre 8.4%, post 12.2% (Figure 1). The surveys differed markedly on measured covariates—e.g., mean BMI 23.7 (KCHS) vs 29.8 (NHANES), and weighted food-insecurity prevalence 2.9% vs ~16%—consistent with genuinely distinct populations and, for food insecurity, with the single-item-vs-module measurement difference. DPQ item non-response was substantial in both NHANES cycles and much larger after the pandemic: among adults ≥20 years, 14.3% of the pre-pandemic and 32.7% of the post-pandemic respondents left three or more PHQ-9 items unanswered, yielding the complete-case analytic samples of 7,888 and 5,210. Non-response was differential—higher among respondents with less than high-school education (20.6% pre, 47.3% post) and among Asian and Black respondents—so the external cohorts, and particularly the post-pandemic fairness estimates, should be read as complete-case results. Reassuringly, adding respondents missing only one or two items using their person-mean changed the observed prevalence by ≤0.1 percentage points in both cycles (unweighted: 9.23%→9.27% pre, 12.69%→12.78% post). Exact values are tabulated in Multimedia Appendix 3, Table S6.
docs/manuscript_17var.md:100:LightGBM was the primary model selected a priori by internal cross-validated AUROC (§2.8). Internal hold-out AUROC was **0.832 (95% CI 0.822–0.841)**, AUPRC 0.233, Brier 0.031, calibration slope 1.00, CITL +0.10 — **H1 met**.
docs/manuscript_17var.md:120:Recalibration is intercept/slope updating applied out-of-sample (5-fold cross-fit); because each fold uses a separate monotone calibration map, the recalibrated AUROC may differ trivially (by ≤0.003) from the original and is not the target of recalibration (calibration slope/CITL are). Internal calibration uses the original probabilities. Race/ethnicity is used only for the NHANES fairness analysis, not as a predictor.
docs/manuscript_17var.md:125:### 3.5 Why external discrimination plateaus at ~0.77: concept shift
docs/manuscript_17var.md:128:We localized the ceiling with an **in-domain oracle**: fitting NHANES on its own labels (a deliberate §2.10 violation, for diagnosis only) under cross-validation. Across logistic, penalized-logistic, histogram-gradient-boosting and LightGBM learners, the honest 5-fold cross-validated oracle reached only 0.765–0.772 (best learner 0.772, 95% CI 0.757–0.787 by stratified cluster bootstrap). The KCHS-transferred model (pre 0.772, post 0.769) therefore already **sits within the maximum discrimination extractable from these predictors within NHANES itself**—the transported model is at, not below, its achievable ceiling. Matching sample size does not close the gap: reducing the Korean data to the NHANES sample size and cross-validating within it still yielded 0.823 at n=7,888 (the pre-pandemic cohort size) and 0.810 at n=13,098 (the pooled oracle size), rising to 0.830 only at n=200,000 (best of three learners, 5-fold weighted AUROC). At identical n, in other words, the same 17 predictors carry 0.81–0.82 of discrimination in Korea against an NHANES ceiling of ~0.77. Three further checks converged on the same conclusion: (i) covariate-shift correction by density-ratio reweighting of the KCHS training sample toward the NHANES covariate distribution (labels unused) *reduced* external AUROC (to 0.766/0.758), indicating the gap is not covariate shift; (ii) increasing the KCHS training set from 0.5M to 2M examples did not raise—and slightly lowered—external discrimination, consistent with source-specific overfitting rather than an estimation-limited ceiling; and (iii) adding every remaining harmonized-but-unused survey item (public-assistance receipt, alcohol-consumption detail, weekend sleep) left external AUROC unchanged (Δ<0.001). Deep tabular learners (a multilayer perceptron, TabNet, and an FT-Transformer) were additionally trained and transferred; all fell below gradient boosting internally and none exceeded it on both external cohorts (Multimedia Appendix 4), consistent with the model-agnostic ceiling. The residual gap thus reflects **concept shift** (predictor–outcome relationships differ between populations), not sample size, model choice, covariate imbalance, or omitted harmonized information, and is not closable by recalibration (which corrects calibration, not rank ordering).
docs/manuscript_17var.md:134:Discrimination was stable across specifications that alter modeling choices rather than the target condition (Figure 4): weighted training (0.831/0.773/0.764 internal/pre/post), missing-indicator imputation (0.833/0.770/0.767), multiple imputation by chained equations (0.833/0.770/0.767), and the 16- and 14-predictor variants (§3.4) all lay within 0.01 of the primary model internally. Restricting both training and evaluation to respondents aged ≥30 years *improved* discrimination (0.840/0.784/0.788), consistent with the lower subgroup performance observed at ages 20–39 (§3.8). Changing the outcome definition to PHQ-9 ≥5 lowered discrimination throughout (0.759/0.736/0.739) at a much higher weighted prevalence (16.1%/25.0%/32.1%), as expected when the target shifts from moderate-or-worse symptoms to any symptom burden; notably, the Korea–US gap narrows from about 0.06 AUROC at the ≥10 threshold to about 0.02 at ≥5 (0.759 vs 0.736–0.739), suggesting that the transportability loss is concentrated at the more severe end of the symptom distribution. Data-integrity fixes (§2.13) did not alter substantive conclusions. Exact values are tabulated in Multimedia Appendix 3, Table S7.
docs/manuscript_17var.md:139:Subgroup discrimination in KCHS was fairly uniform (weighted AUROC 0.785–0.859): sex (female 0.822, 95% CI 0.811–0.833; male 0.831, 0.816–0.845), education (0.813–0.830), and age (20–39 years 0.785, 0.766–0.802 < 40–59 years 0.828, 0.812–0.843 < ≥60 years 0.859, 0.849–0.870). In the NHANES pre-pandemic cohort, discrimination was lower and far less precisely estimated: Mexican American 0.697 (0.646–0.751; n=914), non-Hispanic Black 0.762 (0.738–0.786; n=2,096), other/multiracial 0.772 (0.698–0.840; n=381), other Hispanic 0.777 (0.747–0.808; n=813), non-Hispanic White 0.778 (0.743–0.809; n=2,798), and non-Hispanic Asian 0.810 (0.748–0.857; n=886). These intervals overlap one another and the cohort-level estimate, so the ordering should not be read as established differential performance; the lowest point estimate, in Mexican American respondents, nonetheless warrants monitoring as an equity caveat (Figure 6). Internal subgroup calibration was good (slope 0.97–1.02 across sex and age strata, 0.93–1.03 including education strata), whereas external subgroup calibration slopes were uniformly below 1 (0.58–0.78 across NHANES race/ethnicity groups), mirroring the cohort-level under-prediction that recalibration corrects (§3.3). Exact subgroup values with CIs are tabulated in Multimedia Appendix 3, Table S9.
docs/manuscript_17var.md:141:On decision-curve analysis (weighted, cohort-specific treat-all reference), the model provided positive net benefit across a clinically relevant threshold-probability range in all three datasets (Figure 7). At the Youden-optimal threshold, sensitivity/specificity were 0.75/0.76 (internal), 0.76/0.65 (NHANES pre), and 0.71/0.68 (NHANES post); positive predictive value was low (0.103–0.236) and negative predictive value high (0.945–0.988), as expected for population screening at low prevalence (full operating characteristics across thresholds in Multimedia Appendix 3, Table S1). Tuned to a triage-appropriate 85% sensitivity, the model flagged roughly half of the external population (pre 51%, post 55%) at a PPV of 0.14 (pre) and 0.19 (post)—a number needed to screen of 7.1 and 5.4—and the 8-item deployment model performed comparably (PPV 0.13/0.18; number needed to screen 7.6/5.6). After local intercept/slope recalibration, net benefit remained positive and exceeded both default strategies—screening everyone and screening no one—at threshold probabilities of 0.10–0.15 in every dataset; screen-all net benefit was in fact negative at these thresholds everywhere except NHANES post at 0.10, where the model still added +0.033 (Multimedia Appendix 3, Tables S4–S5). Clinical utility remains provisional pending prospective evaluation.
docs/manuscript_17var.md:148:A depression-screening ML model developed in Korea and externally validated in the US met its internal target (AUROC 0.832) and retained clinically useful—though lower—discrimination (~0.77) on genuinely external US data and across the COVID-19 shift, with calibration recoverable by lightweight recalibration. Expanding the transportable predictor set from 14 to 17 produced a real external gain that survived giving the 14-predictor comparator its own hyperparameter search; further modeling did not, and the external plateau at ~0.77 is explained by concept shift rather than sample size or algorithm.
docs/manuscript_17var.md:151:Prior ML depression models built on Korean national surveys report strong internal performance but no external validation. Using KNHANES 2014–2018 restricted to non-smokers at risk of second-hand smoke exposure (n=11,463), Kim et al [12] classified PHQ-9 ≥10 with internal AUROC up to 0.90; using the 2023 KCHS restricted to culturally diverse families (n=2,568), Kim et al [11] predicted the continuous PHQ-9 score with a test R² of 33%—both identifying perceived stress and subjective health as dominant predictors, and neither attempting external validation. Two deliberate design choices explain why our internal discrimination is lower (0.83): we excluded outcome-proximal predictors such as perceived stress and single-item depressed-mood questions, which inflate apparent performance but are near-tautological with the outcome; and we restricted the predictor set to variables transportable to a second country. In exchange, this study provides what prior work did not—genuine cross-national and cross-pandemic external validation, an explicit transportability ceiling characterized by an in-domain oracle and >450 leakage-controlled experiments, and a recalibration analysis. That the same predictors carry substantially more signal within Korea (~0.83) than in the US (~0.77) is a concrete instance of the dataset-shift problem described for deployed clinical AI [10], and underscores that high internally-reported AUROCs do not guarantee cross-population usefulness.
docs/manuscript_17var.md:157:Cross-sectional (screens current symptoms, not future onset); differing measurement of alcohol, physical activity, income, BMI, and food insecurity; DPQ non-response of 14.3% (pre) and 32.7% (post) handled as complete-case and differential by education and race/ethnicity (§3.1), which may bias the external estimates—especially the post-pandemic subgroup results—in an unknown direction; living-alone unavailable in NHANES pre; food insecurity single-item and unmeasured in KCHS 2020–2021; reduced discrimination in some racial/ethnic subgroups (wide CIs); low PPV at population prevalence (triage aid, not diagnostic); and prior depression diagnosis not recorded in KCHS 2017–2025 (precluding a diagnosis-exclusion sensitivity analysis). Model development used a 500,000-record training set and a 100,000-record hold-out drawn from the 2,048,510 pooled records rather than the full pooled sample (§2.2); a training-set size experiment showed this did not cost external discrimination (§3.5), but internal estimates are hold-out rather than fully cross-validated. The 14-predictor comparator was trained with the same algorithm and a separately tuned configuration (§3.4), yet both models share a single preprocessing and evaluation pipeline, so the 14→17 comparison isolates predictor content rather than modeling strategy. Finally, the 8-item deployment model lost 0.016 AUROC relative to the full model in the post-pandemic cohort (§3.6), so item reduction should be re-evaluated whenever the deployment population differs materially from the development population.
docs/manuscript_17var.md:159:The transportable predictor set is also bounded by the *development* survey's content. In a coverage analysis we extracted candidate domains available in NHANES but not harmonizable to KCHS—physician-diagnosed multimorbidity (asthma, arthritis, thyroid, chronic obstructive pulmonary disease, liver disease, and cancer, excluding circulatory conditions) and healthcare utilization—and found that adding them raised the NHANES in-domain oracle from 0.765 to 0.778. KCHS, however, records only hypertension and diabetes among physician-diagnosed conditions (both already included) and does not collect a broader chronic-disease inventory, hospitalization, or visit counts; these domains therefore cannot enter a KCHS-developed transportable model. Part of the residual external gap is thus attributable to development-survey coverage rather than concept shift alone, and a development survey that captured multimorbidity and utilization could plausibly lift the transportable ceiling by ~0.01 AUROC.
docs/manuscript_17var.md:192:**Figure legends.** Figure 1: Weighted sample characteristics across cohorts—panel A, prevalence/proportion; panel B, means (exact values in Multimedia Appendix 3, Table S6). Figure 2: Calibration plots (internal, NHANES pre, NHANES post; original probabilities). Figure 3: Receiver operating characteristic curves (weighted) for internal, NHANES pre, and NHANES post. Figure 4: Sensitivity analyses—weighted AUROC across specifications for internal, NHANES pre, and NHANES post (exact values in Multimedia Appendix 3, Table S7). Figure 5: Global SHAP importance of the 17 predictors (KCHS). Figure 6: Subgroup discrimination (weighted AUROC) by sex, age, and education (KCHS) and race/ethnicity (NHANES pre). Figure 7: Decision curves (net benefit) for the three datasets. Tables: Table 1 (harmonized predictor set) and Table 2 (discrimination and calibration).
docs/manuscript_17var.md:216:- **Multimedia Appendix 4.** Transportability-ceiling analyses: leakage-controlled optimization attempts and deep tabular learners (Tables S2–S3) and the NHANES in-domain oracle by predictor set.

