  35: ## Table S5. Decision-curve net benefit (after local recalibration)
  36: 
  37: Net benefit at threshold probabilities of 0.10 and 0.15, using intercept/slope?recalibrated probabilities. Two default strategies are shown: screening everyone (screen-all), whose net benefit is negative at these thresholds in every cohort except NHANES post at 0.10, and screening no one (net benefit 0 by definition). A model is clinically useful when it exceeds **both**; the last column reports the margin over whichever default is better. Flag rate is the share referred.
  38: 
  39: | Model | Set | Threshold | Model net benefit | Screen-all NB | vs screen-all | vs better default strategy | Flag rate |
  40: |---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|
  41: | 17-predictor | Internal | 0.10 | +0.0095 | -0.0711 | +0.0806 | +0.0095 | 0.083 |
  42: | 17-predictor | Internal | 0.15 | +0.0063 | -0.1341 | +0.1404 | +0.0063 | 0.048 |
