===== docs/appendix4_ceiling.md =====
   1: # Multimedia Appendix 4. Transportability-ceiling analyses
   2: 
   3: Supporting Results §3.5 (concept-shift ceiling) and Discussion §4.4 (development-survey coverage). All model selection used KCHS-internal criteria only; NHANES labels were used solely for the in-domain oracle diagnostic (a deliberate §2.10 violation, quarantined to this appendix). Weighted AUROC throughout.
   4: 
   5: ## Table S2a. Leakage-controlled attempts to exceed the external ceiling
   6: 
   7: Every learner and adaptation was trained on KCHS and applied to NHANES without refitting (unless noted). None exceeded the pre-specified LightGBM baseline once selection was restricted to KCHS-internal information.
   8: 
   9: | Strategy | External pre | External post | Verdict |
  10: |---|---|---|---|
  11: | **LightGBM baseline (17 predictors, primary)** | **0.772** | **0.769** | reference |
  12: | Add public-assistance receipt (welfare) | 0.772 | 0.769 | no change |
  13: | Add welfare + 12-mo welfare | 0.772 | 0.769 | no change |
  14: | Add welfare + weekend sleep | 0.771 | 0.769 | no change |
  15: | Increase KCHS training 0.5M → 1.0M | 0.768 | 0.768 | lower |
  16: | Increase KCHS training 0.5M → 2.0M | 0.767 | 0.767 | lower |
  17: | 5-seed probability ensemble | 0.769 | 0.768 | ? |
  18: | Logistic-regression transfer | 0.759 | 0.760 | lower |
  19: | Elastic-net transfer | 0.759 | 0.760 | lower |
  20: | LightGBM + logistic blend (w=0.5) | 0.768 | 0.771 | offsetting |
  21: | Covariate-shift density-ratio reweighting | 0.766 | 0.758 | lower |
  22: | Within-survey quantile normalization | 0.762 | 0.773 | offsetting |
  23: 
  24: Two adaptations moved pre and post in opposite directions (blend, quantile normalization); neither yields a net gain selectable without external labels.
  25: 
  26: ## Table S2a-2. Prespecified method search (independent re-run, 300k KCHS training)
  27: 
  28: An additional seven leakage-free strategies were run with an in-run same-sample baseline (pre 0.769, post 0.769). None improved both external cohorts. Notably, a leave-one-year-out (LOYO) transfer-robustness search re-selected the primary hyperparameters, and a transport-stability feature search retained all predictors?independent confirmations that the deployed model is already well specified. Maximizing internal cross-validation (M7) raised pre but lowered post, illustrating that internal optimization does not transfer consistently.
  29: 
  30: | Method | External pre | External post | Verdict |
  31: |---|---|---|---|
  32: | Baseline (300k) | 0.769 | 0.769 | reference |
  33: | Monotonic constraints (clinical direction) | 0.761 | 0.746 | lower |
  34: | Transport-stable feature selection (PSI; internal-selected k=0) | 0.769 | 0.769 | no change |
  35: | LOYO transfer-robust hyperparameters (re-selected baseline) | 0.769 | 0.769 | no change |
  36: | KCHS per-year model ensemble | 0.765 | 0.766 | lower |
  37: | Out-of-fold weight-of-evidence target encoding | 0.769 | 0.770 | no change |
  38: | Missing-indicator augmentation | 0.768 | 0.769 | no change |
  39: | Explicit imputation (no tree-native missing) | 0.769 | 0.768 | no change |
  40: | Internal-CV-tuned configuration | 0.772 | 0.767 | offsetting (pre↑, post↓) |
  41: 
  42: ## Table S2a-3. Deep tabular learners
  43: 
  44: Four neural architectures were trained on KCHS and transferred without refitting, with model/architecture selection by KCHS-internal validation only. All fell below gradient boosting internally (0.837) and none exceeded it on both external cohorts?consistent with the model-agnostic in-domain ceiling and with the established advantage of gradient boosting on modest-signal tabular data.
  45: 
  46: | Deep model | Internal | External pre | External post | Verdict |
  47: |---|---|---|---|---|
  48: | Gradient boosting (reference) | 0.837 | 0.769 | 0.769 | reference |
  49: | Multilayer perceptron (internal-selected) | 0.833 | 0.768 | 0.773 | not better |
  50: | TabNet | 0.829 | 0.755 | 0.766 | lower |
  51: | Deep MLP (BatchNorm+Dropout, PyTorch) | 0.835 | 0.765 | 0.776 | offsetting |
  52: | FT-Transformer | 0.836 | 0.763 | 0.772 | offsetting |
  53: 
  54: ## Table S3. NHANES in-domain oracle by predictor set
  55: 
  56: Fit and cross-validated within NHANES on its own labels (5-fold, pooled pre+post; maximum of penalized-logistic and gradient-boosting learners). This estimates the maximum discrimination extractable from a predictor set *within* NHANES ? an upper bound the transported model cannot exceed.
  57: 
  58: | Predictor set | Pooled oracle AUROC | Δ vs 17 |
  59: |---|---|---|
  60: | 17 harmonized predictors | 0.765 | ? |
  61: | 17 + insurance coverage | 0.766 | +0.000 |
  62: | 17 + multimorbidity count | 0.774 | +0.009 |
  63: | 17 + healthcare utilization (visits, hospitalization) | 0.771 | +0.006 |
  64: | 17 + multimorbidity + utilization + insurance | **0.778** | **+0.012** |
  65: 
  66: The transported LightGBM model (pre 0.772, post 0.769) already meets the 17-predictor oracle, confirming the external number is at?not below?its achievable ceiling for this predictor set.
  67: 
  68: ### Table S3b. Oracle by learner, with confidence intervals
  69: 
  70: Pooled pre+post NHANES, fitted and 5-fold cross-validated on its own labels; 95% CIs from the stratified cluster bootstrap (1000 replicates, PSUs resampled within period-specific design strata). An in-sample (apparent) bound is not reported because it is strongly learner-dependent (0.77 for a heavily regularized learner, 0.83 for a more flexible one) and therefore carries no information about the ceiling.
  71: 
  72: | Learner | Oracle AUROC (5-fold CV) | 95% CI |
  73: |---|---|---|
  74: | Logistic regression | 0.765 | 0.748?0.782 |
  75: | Penalized logistic (L2, C=0.1) | 0.765 | 0.749?0.782 |
  76: | Histogram gradient boosting | 0.767 | 0.751?0.783 |
  77: | LightGBM (small) | 0.772 | 0.757?0.787 |
  78: 
  79: ## Interpretation
  80: 
  81: - The 17-predictor external ceiling (~0.77) is a **concept-shift** limit: the transported model is already oracle-optimal for these predictors, and covariate-shift correction does not help (it lowers AUROC), indicating the predictor?outcome relationship, not the covariate distribution, differs across populations.
  82: - Multimorbidity and healthcare utilization carry additional, transportable signal in NHANES (oracle 0.765→0.778). These domains are **not collected by KCHS** (only hypertension and diabetes are recorded as physician-diagnosed conditions across all survey years, both already included), so they cannot enter a KCHS-developed transportable model. Part of the residual ceiling is therefore development-survey coverage, not concept shift alone.
  83: 
  84: *Multimorbidity count comprised physician-diagnosed asthma, arthritis, thyroid disorder, chronic obstructive pulmonary disease, liver disease, and cancer (circulatory conditions excluded per the prespecified predictor policy; mental-health service contact excluded as tautological). Healthcare-visit count (HUQ051) is unavailable in the NHANES August 2021?2023 file and was carried as pre-cycle only.*
