docs/manuscript_17var.md:19:**Results.** Gradient boosting (LightGBM) was the primary model. Internal AUROC was 0.832 (95% CI 0.822–0.841), meeting the ≥0.80 target (H1). External discrimination remained clinically useful but lower (pre 0.772, 95% CI 0.745–0.797; post 0.769, 0.747–0.789); calibration deteriorated (slope 0.66–0.68; calibration-in-the-large +0.49 and +1.11, ie, systematic under-prediction) and was fully restored by recalibration (slope→0.99, CITL→0.00; H2, H3 supported). Against an independently tuned 14-predictor model, the three added predictors improved external discrimination (pre 0.749→0.772, Δ+0.023; post 0.753→0.769, Δ+0.016; both *P*<.001). An 8-item model retained near-full external discrimination pre-pandemic (0.770 vs 0.772) but lost 0.016 post-pandemic (0.753 vs 0.769), partially supporting H4. Extensive leakage-controlled optimization (>450 experiments) did not exceed ~0.77 externally; an in-domain oracle (fitting NHANES on its own labels) capped at ~0.77, whereas the same predictors reached 0.81–0.82 in Korean samples of the same size (0.83 at full scale), indicating concept shift rather than sample size or model choice.
docs/manuscript_17var.md:37:We pursued four aims: (1) develop an ML depression-screening model in KCHS; (2) externally validate it in US NHANES (geographic transportability); (3) validate it separately pre/post-COVID-19 and quantify recalibration (temporal transportability); and (4) derive a parsimonious model for a low-burden digital tool. We hypothesized: **H1** the full model achieves internal AUROC ≥0.80; **H2** external discrimination remains clinically useful while calibration may deteriorate; **H3** recalibration substantially improves calibration; **H4** the parsimonious model incurs only a small performance loss.
docs/manuscript_17var.md:100:LightGBM was the primary model selected a priori by internal cross-validated AUROC (§2.8). Internal hold-out AUROC was **0.832 (95% CI 0.822–0.841)**, AUPRC 0.233, Brier 0.031, calibration slope 1.00, CITL +0.10 — **H1 met**.
docs/manuscript_17var.md:103:See Table 2 and Figures 2 and 3. Discrimination remained clinically useful but lower: pre **0.772 (0.745–0.797)**, post **0.769 (0.747–0.789)**. Calibration deteriorated in both directions of the calibration line: the slope fell to 0.66 (pre) and 0.68 (post), and CITL rose to +0.49 and +1.11, indicating that the Korea-trained model systematically *under*-predicted absolute risk in the US—as expected from the large prevalence difference (KCHS 3.6% vs NHANES 8.4% and 12.2%). Out-of-sample intercept/slope recalibration restored calibration (slope→0.99, CITL→0.00) with discrimination essentially unchanged — **H2 and H3 supported**.
docs/manuscript_17var.md:131:Predictors were entered in descending order of global SHAP importance and the model refitted at each item count (internal / external-pre / external-post): 3 items 0.782/0.713/0.726; 5 items 0.809/0.741/0.745; 6 items 0.815/0.752/0.754; 7 items 0.819/0.761/0.758; **8 items 0.825/0.770/0.753**; 12 items 0.831/0.771/0.767; 17 items 0.832/0.772/0.769. Eight items was the smallest count whose internal discrimination came within 0.01 AUROC of the full model (0.825 vs 0.832; 7 items fell short at 0.819), and this criterion used internal data only. The resulting 8-item model (self-rated health, age, sex, sleep duration, oral health, partnership, employment, smoking) retained essentially all external discrimination in the pre-pandemic cohort (0.770 vs 0.772) but lost 0.016 in the post-pandemic cohort (0.753 vs 0.769); **H4 is therefore supported for the pre-pandemic cohort and only partially for the post-pandemic cohort**, where a 12-item version recovers the difference (0.767). The 8-item version remains suitable for a low-burden self-administered form, with the post-pandemic shortfall reported as a deployment caveat. The full item-count trajectory is tabulated in Multimedia Appendix 3, Table S8.
